values
  ('cdff2833-b7fb-57a2-bb91-5dcbb124b87c', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'development', null, null, 'Overview', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '3 - 1. Civil Affairs Soldiers are expected to succeed in missions that are often demanding and high-risk within an increasingly complex and dynamic contemporary operating environment. Therefore, the CA branch naturally produces a high concentration of highly skilled NCOs. NCOs that consistently exceed standards and demonstrate mental and physical capabilities above their peers are more competitive for demanding positions and career advancement. The Special Operations Center of Excellence, the CA Proponent, the United States Army Human Resources Command (HRC), and the NCO Professional Development System (NCOPDS) all play an important part in the career development of the force; however, each Soldier is ultimately the true steward of their career. To assist leaders with talent management, NCOs at every level must seek opportunities to demonstrate their leadership potential and distinguish themselves from their peers. Refer to DA PAM 600-25 (U.S. Army Noncommissioned Officer Professional Development Guide), para 2-15. (https://www.army.mil/g-1#org-g-1-publications) for more on Self-development. 3 - 2. Physical Fitness. The ability to conduct CAO across the competition continuum requires CA Soldiers to be physically capable of operating in the most rigorous environments. All personnel must maintain a high state of readiness. Physical fitness contributes to NCOs overall wellness and ability to set the example for others. A holistic health and fitness (H2F) approach is essential to readiness when assessing a Soldier’s physical and mental status. Additionally, it assists to design programs to sustain or improve that Soldier’s ability to meet the demands of their MOSs, duty assignments, and combat-specific tasks. While it is a command responsibility to establish physical training policies and allocate sufficient resources for Soldiers to meet Army and unit standards and goals, it is an individual responsibility to meet those goals. Soldiers must keep themselves in a high state of physical readiness. All Soldiers are encouraged to use existing resources to assist them in making a healthy lifestyle, good dietary choices, and developing an individual goal-oriented physical training regimen. CA Soldiers will meet all standards for their respective grades as specified in DA PAM 611-21. NCOs assigned to either the Active Component or Reserve Components must meet all physical fitness requirements as directed by their command to ensure maximum mission readiness to fulfill global requirements with Joint Special Operations Forces (SOF) partners and support Joint Commanders. 3 - 3. Language: Your Strategic Advantage.
(1) Fluency equals operational success: As a Civil Affairs NCO in a language-capable CMF, your language proficiency is mission-critical. Achieve and maintain the highest level of fluency possible for your assigned control language (CLANG).
(2) Cultural Expertise Amplifies Impact: Combine your language skills with a deep understanding of regional cultures, customs, values, and social norms. This enables you to connect meaningfully, build trust, and navigate complex situations.
(3) Stay Current, Stay Relevant: The world is constantly evolving. Continuously update your knowledge of regional history, current events, and emerging trends to anticipate challenges and capitalize on opportunities.
(4) Communication is Your Weapon: Leverage your language and cultural expertise to engage local populations, gather critical information, influence perceptions, and decisively achieve mission objectives.
a. Active-duty CA NCOs are required to maintain control language proficiency in accordance with AR 11-6 Army Foreign Language Program, AR 614-200 Enlisted Assignments and Utilization Management, USASOC Regulation 350-1, and applicable unit policies. Soldiers scoring under the L3/R3 DLPT level or S3 OPI level must be recertified annually. Those who score L3/R3 on the DLPT or S3 on the OPI have a biennial (every 2-years) mandatory recertification requirement. Commanders must adhere to the policies identified in this chapter to re-evaluate their assigned ARSOF Soldiers’ proficiency IAW AR 11-6 dated 25 February 2022, Chapter 5-4. Army Special Operations Forces language proficiency testing.
b. Language capable ARSOF MOSs 38Z, 38R, 38W while serving as Drill Sergeants, Recruiters, and Observer Controllers are exempt from the 12-month language currency for 36- months while serving in these positions IAW Exception to Policy Memorandum dated 07 January 2020 signed by HQDAG-2 Language Team.
c. USAR members are encouraged to seek a language through the Defense Language Institute Foreign Language Center (DLIFLC) but are not required to do so in accordance with AR 11-6. Many USAR CA positions are specifically language coded based on the unit of assignment. Training may be authorized through the DLIFLC or numerous other approved programs and venues. USAR Soldiers that have received language training are required to attend 80 hours of consecutive language training or equivalent annually. All 38B Soldiers that meet minimum proficiency standards for any foreign language are authorized Foreign Language Proficiency Pay. 3 - 4. Military Bearing. Soldiers in all ranks must maintain a high level of physical, moral, and ethical discipline. NCOs in all grades should be well-versed in Army customs, courtesies, and traditions in accordance with: AR 600-25 (Salutes, Honors, and Courtesies), AR 670-1 (Wear and Appearance of Army Uniforms and Insignia), TC 3-21.5 (Drill and Ceremonies), TC 7-21.13 (Soldier’s Guide), TC 7-22.7 (The Noncommissioned Officer Guide), and applicable unit policies. 3 - 5. Required attributes. Soldiers wishing to obtain and maintain an Active Component ARSOF CA MOS (38Z, 38R or 38W) must demonstrate and internalize the following ARSOF core attributes:
a. Integrity. Be trustworthy and honest, act with honor and unwavering adherence to ethical standards.
b. Courage. Act on own convictions despite consequences, willing to sacrifice for a larger cause, be not paralyzed by fear of failure.
c. Perseverance. Work toward an end, have commitment, physical or mental resolve, motivated, give effort to the cause, do not quit.
d. Personal Responsibility. Be self-motivated and an autonomous self-starter, anticipate tasks and act, accordingly, take accountability for his/her actions.
e. Professionalism. Be a standard-bearer for the corps, have a professional image, to include a level of maturity and judgment mixed with confidence and humility, form sound opinions and make own decisions, stand behind his/her sensible decisions based on his/her experiences.
f. Adaptability. Personnel must demonstrate a high degree of adaptability and cognitive flexibility, maintaining composure while adjusting to dynamic environments. They are expected to employ creative problem-solving and think unconventionally to overcome challenges. A critical skill is the ability to recognize, understand, and navigate complex social networks within the human domain. Finally, CA professionals must exhibit the initiative to proactively shape the operational environment and set conditions to achieve desired outcomes.
g. Team Player. Demonstrates unwavering loyalty to the team and selfless dedication to the shared mission. Dependable, driven by a strong sense of duty to their fellow team members and the task at hand. By treating everyone with dignity and respect, they foster cohesion and recognize that the diverse strengths within the team are essential to mission success and serve as a force multiplier.
h. Capability. Maintain physical fitness, to include strength and agility, maintain operational knowledge, able to plan and communicate effectively. 3 - 6. Functional Training. Civil Affairs NCOs are highly encouraged to pursue formal military training opportunities that improve their survivability, tactical, and technical skills and increase their ability to lead Soldiers in any CA unit that they may be assigned. This includes but is not limited to all Additional Skill Identifier (ASI) and Special Qualifications Identifier (SQI) Producing Schools, Joint Service Courses, and International Military/Government Training.
a. Due to the high risk of isolation within potential denied areas inherent to the operational environment in which active-duty CATs operate, units are required to provide Survival, Evasion, Resistance, and Escape (SERE-C High Risk) for every active-duty CA Soldier IAW AR 525-28 para.2-21. Additionally, over 85% of Critical Leadership Development opportunities in the active component are airborne assignments, therefore all active-duty CA NCOs should successfully complete the Static Line Jumpmaster Course at the earliest opportunity.
b. USAR CA Forces support the conventional force with a structure that includes two Airborne BDEs and subordinate BNs and other Airborne coded positions. Individuals in these assignments must attend the Basic Airborne Course and maintain current jump status to be fully qualified for the position. Prior to assignment in a paid parachute position, non-airborne qualified TPU and AGR Soldiers should complete an Airborne Training Volunteer Statement. All NCOs in an airborne assignment should attend the Static Line Jump Master Course at the earliest opportunity. USAR Soldiers should also pursue job specific and functional courses that enhance professional technical skills and knowledge at various schools including the Army Reserve Readiness Training Center (ARRTC). Unless directed, USAR Soldiers do not have a SERE-C requirement. 3 - 7. Additional Military Educational Opportunities. Soldiers of all ranks are highly encouraged to continue their military training and education through self-paced and distributed-learning platforms. Courses in a wide variety of military topics can be found at the Army Training Information System (ATIS) Learning, Joint Knowledge Online (JKO), and Joint Special Operations University.', '9f27e9104f4abfa3cacf1a35e0d91ea660c165b1db6da47d986e994dd44426e8', 5, 7, null, 0, null, null, false, null),
  ('4e65e5b6-8493-5e9b-a1c1-0c378d3980d1', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'development', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private-Specialist/Corporal — recommended professional military education (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', 'Decentralized promotion eligibility requirements for USAR Soldiers in the ranks of PVT, PV2, PFC, and SPC are outlined in AR 600-8-19, chapter 2.', '09c82084e48da7cd471be051b8dad473bc2e50406514bd85983510ff69ef7b46', 8, 8, null, 1, null, null, false, null),
  ('ce3ea563-242d-5b5d-9b81-3d5e51cedbb1', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'development', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — recommended professional military education (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', 'Consideration by a local promotion board is mandatory for all Soldiers upon initially reaching primary zone eligibility.', 'e91be1813c1d62927b40eb321fe5f9af2912b38c4611e2ec983b85b0ec4eb022', 8, 8, null, 2, null, null, false, null),
  ('ab50ddde-4946-51e9-b03f-01703bccd066', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'development', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — recommended professional military education (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', 'Active Component and AGR Staff Sergeants serving in a SOF unit should strive to complete the Joint Special Operations University Joint Fundamentals Course (CEP 1) and the JKO Enlisted Joint Professional Military Education Course (EJPME I). Staff Sergeants must take the initiative to become familiar with the Army Training Network and the Army Training Information System.', '8d3bfae76552c678c7759d9799d7802126efc414ab1fd29870f4d7333140bdab', 8, 8, null, 3, null, null, false, null),
  ('544af6bb-a28c-5460-9cb7-62eb85176473', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — recommended professional military education (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', 'Active Component and AGR Sergeants First Class serving in a SOF unit should strive to complete the Joint Special Operations University Enterprise Management Course (CEP 2) and the JKO Enlisted Joint Professional Military Education Course (EJPME II).', '48400fe1cf2e22ec7b333e457ea9ce1d8d6f5550c399124e5c151d8384d1ce84', 8, 8, null, 4, null, null, false, null),
  ('1dde1934-3af6-5dbc-9d67-fd4115856def', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant — recommended professional military education (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', 'Master Sergeants and First Sergeants should strive to complete the JKO Enlisted Joint Professional Military Education Course (EJPME II) if not already complete.', 'e35ac6c23beb48d51ba6f0074a509453cb0dc9bfbfd8cf634cd1d70a107f780c', 8, 8, null, 5, null, null, false, null),
  ('6409c3e7-6b61-5da0-bdfa-aef0f3427eb4', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'development', null, null, 'General guidance (broadening billets, competitive boards, education resources, professional reading)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '3 - 8. Broadening billets and assignments. This covers a wide range of positions and duties outside of one’s MOS. CA Enlisted and NCO assignments are classified into three basic categories: Critical Leadership Developmental (CD), Developmental, and Broadening. All assignments are developmental.
a. Critical Leadership assignments are vital for professional development, providing a foundational understanding of CA operations while serving as the framework to assess a Soldier''s potential for career advancement.
b. Developmental assignments are those assignments that enhance warfighting capability but are not deemed so fundamental to CA Soldiers or NCOs progression that they qualify as a Critical Leadership assignment.
c. Broadening assignments are those assignments that provide a purposeful expansion of knowledge and capability and are fundamental to the continued growth throughout the CA Soldiers or NCOs career lifecycle. For any position to be considered broadening, it must either be an operational or institutional position in an organization at the operational or strategic levels providing an experience above and beyond the tactical level or an MOS-immaterial position that increases skill in Army policy and programs outside of their CMF. Assignments and billets include, but are not limited to, assignments at a Theater Special Operations Command TSOC, GCC, USASOC, 1st SFC (A), Multi-Domain Effects Battalion (MDEB), Multi-Domain Task Force (MDTF), Theater Information Advantage Detachment (TIAD), Division or CORP G-9, SFG S-9, OSC or positions such as Observer Controller/Trainer (JRTC/NTC), Drill Sergeant, Recruiter (including SORB), Inspector General (IG) NCO, Brigade level Sexual Assault Response Coordinator or Sexual Harassment/Assault Response and Prevention Coordinator, and EOA assignments. Broadening often occurs following Critical Leadership assignments. The full list of assignments can be found in the paragraphs below for each grade plate. To be considered ''Best Qualified'' and competitive for future promotions, CA Soldiers must complete their Critical Leadership assignment, and all required professional military education at each grade. 3 - 9. Competitive Boards. Soldiers at all ranks are encouraged to surpass standards in presence, intellect, leadership, development, and achievement, setting the example for their peers and subordinates through courage, confidence, competence, military bearing, general military knowledge, and warrior skills. Those seeking to challenge themselves and excel beyond their peers should consider competing in events such as: Active Component Best Civil Affairs Competition, U.S. Army Best Medic Competition, U.S. Army Best Ranger Competition, U.S. Army Best Sapper Competition, U.S. Army Best Warrior/Best Squad Competition, Soldier and NCO of the Month, Quarter, and Year boards. NCOs at all ranks are encouraged to seek acceptance into the prestigious Sergeant Audie Murphy or Sergeant Morales Clubs. 3 - 10. Credentials/Certifications. Must maintain a current, valid, unrestricted National Registry Emergency Medical Technician Certification and Advanced Tactical Paramedic Certification to retain MOS 38W or W4 ASI. 3 - 11. Civilian Education. Civilian education is a vital component of self-development for Civil Affairs Soldiers. Prioritizing education early in your career accelerates professional growth and enhances flexibility in navigating operational demands. Although education is an important factor enhancing potential value to the Army, comparison of SMs based on civilian education attainments should be viewed as an indicator of individual initiative rather than as a prerequisite for selection as most/highly qualified. College is favorable to set SMs apart from their peers, but it is not a requirement for promotion. Soldiers should pursue degree programs relevant to their MOS to maximize credit for prior military training. Explore the Service-member Opportunity Colleges Army Degree (SOCAD) website for more information, https://www.education.army.mil/socad.aspx. For details on obtaining college credits, refer to paragraph 10.
a. Staff Sergeants should aim to complete an associate degree, or at a minimum complete 60 semester hours. Staff Sergeants that have already completed a bachelor’s degree are highly competitive to pursue a master’s degree with. Civil Affairs Medical Sergeants (38W) students are enrolled into the Uniformed Services University (USU) College of Allied Health Sciences (CAHS) upon admittance to the Special Operation Medical Sergeants Course. Upon graduation of the Special Operations Combat Medical Course, a medic is awarded the Associates of Science (AS) in Health Science from the Uniformed Services University (USU). The primary location of training within USASOC is the Joint Special Operations Medical Training Center.
b. Sergeants First Class should have already completed an associate degree or 90 semester hours by 13 years TIS and should have had the opportunity to work towards or complete their bachelor’s degree. Sergeants First Class who have already completed a bachelor’s degree are highly encouraged to pursue a master’s degree with National Defense University (NDU).
c. Proponent note. National Defense University (NDU). Selected NCOs attend the highly competitive Joint Special Operations Master of Arts program conducted over ten months at Fort Bragg, NC, producing a Master of Arts in Strategic Security Studies. This education better prepares CA NCOs for assignments in Army Educational Requirements System (AERS) coded “98” billets. The gaining command has the responsibility to ensure NCOs assigned against validated AERS positions are properly utilized. Per the regulation (AR 621-1 para 4-5a). Graduates of the program have enhanced strategic situational awareness and are better prepared for the complexities of special operations in interagency and multinational contexts. The application for NDU requires endorsement by the first O-6 in the officer’s chain of command. Graduates of this venue incur a one to two ratio utilization duration tour per AR 621-1 and AR 350-100.
d. Proponent note. NCOs who want to attend must have completed appropriate Noncommissioned Officer educational programs for grade and years of service. NCOs normally must have no less than 6 years-time in service or no more than 13 years-time in service (TIS) as of the report date to the academic institution. NCOs with more than 13-year TIS (waiver possible) will be considered on a case-by-case basis. Must be indefinite or have sufficient enlistment remaining equal to or greater than three times the length of requested schooling. Must be in the grade of E7 or above.
e. Master Sergeants and First Sergeants should have at least 120 semester hours of civilian college education towards a bachelor or higher-level degree. There are certain positions or billets that may require a master’s degree to apply. Master Sergeants and First Sergeants should complete a bachelor’s degree and be willing to work towards a master’s degree while serving in the assigned billet.
f. Sergeants Major and Command Sergeants Major should have a bachelor’s degree and are highly encouraged to obtain a master’s degree. Joint Special Operations University courses, especially CEP 4 (Enlisted Academy Summit course) will become available if selected to serve at the nominative level. Due to limited authorizations and fiercely competitive records, civilian education may be considered a major factor for selection to Command Sergeant Major (CSM). There are certain positions or billets that may require a master’s degree to apply. At a minimum, Sergeants Major must have completed a bachelor’s degree and be willing to work towards a master’s degree while serving in a AERS 98 coded billet. 3 - 12. College Opportunities. Many opportunities and resources exist to support CA Soldiers in pursuing their civilian education: Army Continuing Education System, Army University, Defense Activity for Non-Traditional Education Support, Army IgnitED, Joint Service Transcript, and Advanced Civil Schooling opportunities. Achievement of college or higher-level degree credits can also be completed during the MOS training process, as modeled by the 38W SOCM program degree.
a. Army Continuing Education System (ACES). ACES programs and services support the self- development domain of the Army leader development tenets by providing Soldiers opportunities for traditional and technology-based education and support for personal and professional career goals. The ACES programs assist Soldiers in achieving the lifelong synthesis of the knowledge, skills, and experiences required for success. Enlisted Soldiers should master the academic skills needed to perform the duties of their primary military occupational specialty and to meet prerequisites for the Noncommissioned Officer Professional Development System (NCOPDS) in accordance with AR 621-5.
b. The DOD MWR Libraries virtual collection includes e-books, audiobooks, academic support, test prep, professional development, and a vast array of learning resources and online content. This platform provides free access to resources including but not limited to, Mango Languages, Universal Calss, EBSCO Learning Express, Gale OneFile: Military and Intelligence, and World Book Student. You can access these materials by going to https://army.dodmwrlibraries.org and logging in with your DoWID number and birth date.
c. Army University. The Army University is a professional military education university system of the United States Army. It is the most extensive professional military education system globally with over 150,000 soldiers educated in more than 88 occupations across its worldwide network of 70 schools. https://armyuniversity.edu/.
d. Defense Activity for Non-Traditional Education Support. Soldiers are encouraged to earn additional college credit for knowledge acquired outside of the traditional classroom through the Defense Activity for Non-Traditional Education Support (DANTES) https://www.dantes.mil/. College Level Examination Program (CLEP) and DANTES Subject Standardized Tests (DSST) are exams that test the mastery of college-level subjects acquired a variety of ways–through general academic instructions, significant independent study, or extracurricular work.
e. Army IgnitED. Army IgnitED is a virtual gateway for all eligible Soldiers to request college tuition assistance or funding for professional credentials and certificates online for classroom and distance learning. It allows Soldiers to manage their education records including college classes, testing, and Army Education Counselor support. Through Online programs, Education Centers and On-site programs offer over 300,000+ college courses. Credentialing Opportunity Online Learning (COOL). COOL helps Army service members find information on certifications and licenses related to their military occupation and civilian career. This assists in the operational aspect when working with unified action partners. There are 35-line areas of certifications for the CA NCO that are applicable to their skills. https://www.cool.osd.mil/army/index.html.
f. Joint Service Transcript. Joint Service Transcript is an academically accepted document approved by the American Council on Education (ACE) to validate a service member''s military occupational experience and training along with the corresponding ACE college credit recommendations. Joint Service Transcript can be accessed at https://jst.doded.mil/jst/home.
g. Advanced Civil Schooling. Advanced Civil Schooling opportunities are available for active component CA NCOs. The National Defense University (NDU) Joint Special Operations Master of Arts Strategic Security Studies degree program is conducted under the auspices of the U.S. Army John F. Kennedy Special Warfare Center and School (USAJFKSWCS) provides a Master of Science. 38Zs with a minimum of six years’ Time in Service (TIS) and no more than 13 years TIS are eligible. Application requirements are published annually.
h. Proponent note. NCOs who received an advanced degree through an Army civilian schooling program are required to serve in an initial AERS validated position for a minimum of 24 months per AR 621-1 MAY 2024. 3 - 13. Professional Reading. Civil Affairs Soldiers of all ranks should pursue self-development and understanding of the Army profession through professional reading. The Sergeant Major of the Army reading list, published by the Association of the United States Army NCO reading list broken down by rank: https://www.ausa.org/nco-reading-list and https://www.ausa.org/nco- soldier-programs. 3 - 14. Guided Self-development by rank', 'eb49f1cc003e37b7eddf256d198e6d91c7707b2393f357bd6b6a21ce9e845f81', 8, 11, null, 6, null, null, false, null),
  ('09c0f5ad-e2a3-5c23-817c-fc5002fa0c01', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'development', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private-Specialist/Corporal (USAR only) — recommended publications and reading (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '(1) All Skill Level 1 CA Specialists should focus on gaining familiarization with Army and CA CMF terminology, and operations at the company and team level. Soldiers should study and master the following military publications: TC 7–21.13 (Soldier’s Guide), STP 21–1–SMCT (Soldier’s Manual of Common Task, SL1), FM 7–22 (Holistic Health and Fitness (H2F)), TC 4- 02.1 (First Aid), AR 670–1 (Wear and Appearance of Army Uniforms and Insignia), TM 9-1005- 319-10 (Operators Manual for Carbine 5.56mm, M4A1), TM 9-1005 - 470-10 (Operators Manual for MHS: M17, M18 Pistol), FM 3-25.26 (Map Reading and Land Navigation), all -10 level technical manuals associated with their equipment, and battle drills associated with their current assignment.
(2) Recommended Reading: Small Unit Leadership: A Commonsense Approach by Dandridge Malone, A Bell for Adano by John Hersey, Ender’s Game by Orson Scott Card, How to Win Friends and Influence People by Dale Carnegie.', 'dabeef07d046d397ae729b0b91136efe155308dec8455cc7ff33a5f56d1f167b', 12, 12, null, 7, null, null, false, null),
  ('07748e9a-164b-58b7-8c1c-1566252ce955', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'development', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant (USAR only) — recommended publications and reading (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '(1) Foundational: TC 7-22.7 (Noncommissioned Officer Guide), STP 21-24-SMCT (Warrior Tasks, SL 2-4), FM 3-57 (Civil Affairs Operations), ADP 1 (The Army), ADP 3-07 (Stability), AR 623-3 (Evaluation Reporting System), ADP 6-22 (Army Leadership and the Profession), AR 600-9 (The Army Body Composition Program), DA PAM 600-25 (base document and Civil Affairs Chapter), ADP 7-0 (Training) and ADP 6-0 (Mission Command).
(2) Recommended Reading: The Ugly American, Starship Troopers, Influence: The Psychology of Persuasion, Savage Wars of Peace.', '2c40a8fba57146b5a23004c27c981ddb74d179e4df956979c191f3e7b2ff2cfd', 12, 12, null, 8, null, null, false, null),
  ('9e854041-3546-5ca6-9013-f05f05e4759f', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'development', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — recommended publications and reading (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '(1) Builds Upon Sergeant: JP 3-57 (Civil-Military Operations), FM 1-02.2 (Military Symbols), FM 1-02.1 (Operational Terms), ADP 3-0 (Operations), ADP 3-05 (Army Special Operations), ADP 3-37 (Protection), ADP 7-0 (Training), FM 3-98 (Reconnaissance and Security Operations), FM 3-90-2 (Reconnaissance, Security, and Tactical Enabling Tasks Volume 2), ATP 6-22.01 (The Counseling Process), ATP 3-50.20 (SERE Planning and Preparation), ADP 5-0 (The Operations Process) for early exposure to operational planning.
(2) Recommended Reading: Talking to Strangers, Inside a U.S. Embassy: Diplomacy at Work, Leaders Eat Last, David and Goliath, The Road Not Taken, The Undoing Project, Team of Teams, This Kind of War.', '3ca98f844ed6c131a7398b20da528819a9fd3a15f612cdce15f609539a6508ad', 12, 12, null, 9, null, null, false, null),
  ('563bd708-a055-58da-be94-ce2e5e247d14', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — recommended publications and reading (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '(1) Builds Upon Staff Sergeant: FM 6-0 (Commander and Staff Organization and Operations) or ADP 6-0 (Mission Command), FM 3-0 (Operations), ADP 5-0 (The Operations Process), ATP 5- 19 (Risk Management), ATP 3-60 (Targeting), FM 7-22.7 (The Noncommissioned Officer Guide), TC 3-57.51 (Civil Preparation of the Battlefield), FM 7-0 (Training), FM 7-22 (Holistic Health and Fitness), FM 6-22 (Developing Leaders), FM 3-13 (Information Operations), FM 3-55 (Information Collection), ATP 2-01.3 (Intelligence Preparation of the Battlefield), AR 25-50 (Preparing and Managing Correspondence), ADP 1-01 (Doctrine Primer), ADP 2-0 (Intelligence), ADP 3-90 (Offense and Defense), ADP 4-0 (Sustainment), ATP 3-57.X series, AR 600-8-22 (Military Awards) and AR 27-10 (Military Justice).
(2) Recommended Reading: Start with Why, Outliers, The Black Swan, World Order, The Training Ground, The Third Pillar, The Generals, The History of the Peloponnesian War, The 4 Disciplines of Execution, The Prince.', 'a90cebeda2e7df3171fb47ce8ae18091fa86164b0d278421cceb69dfd6db3b1b', 12, 13, null, 10, null, null, false, null),
  ('578dde00-9309-58de-b55f-8ca79f86c1f0', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant and First Sergeant — recommended publications and reading (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '(1) Builds Upon Sergeant First Class: AR 600-20 (Army Command Policy), FM 7-22 (Army Physical Readiness Training), AR 11-6 (Army Foreign Language Program), AR 15-185 (Army Board for Correction of Military Records), AR 220-1 (Army Unit Status Reporting), AR 601-280 (Army Retention Program), AR 614-200 (Enlisted Assignments and Utilization), Mastery of Army Training Network.
(2) Recommended Reading: Winning, Becoming a Resonant Leader, Mindset, Good to Great.', '399a821dc9bf14796808069fedef8c341712274cef656255af4d7eaeaa67718a', 13, 13, null, 11, null, null, false, null),
  ('c7f6c96c-2acc-51da-88b0-e561b7c316b6', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'development', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major and Command Sergeant Major — recommended publications and reading (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '(1) Sergeants Major and Command Sergeants Major should continue to study and master regulations, policies, and doctrine as updates are published.
(2) Recommended professional reading for the CA Sergeant Major is: Once an Eagle by Anton Myrer, Thinking Fast and Slow by Daniel Kahneman, The Slight Edge: Turning Simple Disciplines into Massive Success and Happiness by Jeff Olson and John David Mann, Nudge: Improving Decisions About Health, Wealth, and Happiness by Richard M. Thaler and Cass R Sunstein.', '06cf8a73620c0a42a287bb612674d6c85280d3b701829ae4c4ea8be0867d4ceb', 13, 13, null, 12, null, null, false, null),
  ('2798644a-54d1-5824-8682-ba3cc7783a17', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'development', null, null, 'Overview', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '3 - 1. Civil Affairs Soldiers are expected to succeed in missions that are often demanding and high-risk within an increasingly complex and dynamic contemporary operating environment. Therefore, the CA branch naturally produces a high concentration of highly skilled NCOs. NCOs that consistently exceed standards and demonstrate mental and physical capabilities above their peers are more competitive for demanding positions and career advancement. The Special Operations Center of Excellence, the CA Proponent, the United States Army Human Resources Command (HRC), and the NCO Professional Development System (NCOPDS) all play an important part in the career development of the force; however, each Soldier is ultimately the true steward of their career. To assist leaders with talent management, NCOs at every level must seek opportunities to demonstrate their leadership potential and distinguish themselves from their peers. Refer to DA PAM 600-25 (U.S. Army Noncommissioned Officer Professional Development Guide), para 2-15. (https://www.army.mil/g-1#org-g-1-publications) for more on Self-development. 3 - 2. Physical Fitness. The ability to conduct CAO across the competition continuum requires CA Soldiers to be physically capable of operating in the most rigorous environments. All personnel must maintain a high state of readiness. Physical fitness contributes to NCOs overall wellness and ability to set the example for others. A holistic health and fitness (H2F) approach is essential to readiness when assessing a Soldier’s physical and mental status. Additionally, it assists to design programs to sustain or improve that Soldier’s ability to meet the demands of their MOSs, duty assignments, and combat-specific tasks. While it is a command responsibility to establish physical training policies and allocate sufficient resources for Soldiers to meet Army and unit standards and goals, it is an individual responsibility to meet those goals. Soldiers must keep themselves in a high state of physical readiness. All Soldiers are encouraged to use existing resources to assist them in making a healthy lifestyle, good dietary choices, and developing an individual goal-oriented physical training regimen. CA Soldiers will meet all standards for their respective grades as specified in DA PAM 611-21. NCOs assigned to either the Active Component or Reserve Components must meet all physical fitness requirements as directed by their command to ensure maximum mission readiness to fulfill global requirements with Joint Special Operations Forces (SOF) partners and support Joint Commanders. 3 - 3. Language: Your Strategic Advantage.
(1) Fluency equals operational success: As a Civil Affairs NCO in a language-capable CMF, your language proficiency is mission-critical. Achieve and maintain the highest level of fluency possible for your assigned control language (CLANG).
(2) Cultural Expertise Amplifies Impact: Combine your language skills with a deep understanding of regional cultures, customs, values, and social norms. This enables you to connect meaningfully, build trust, and navigate complex situations.
(3) Stay Current, Stay Relevant: The world is constantly evolving. Continuously update your knowledge of regional history, current events, and emerging trends to anticipate challenges and capitalize on opportunities.
(4) Communication is Your Weapon: Leverage your language and cultural expertise to engage local populations, gather critical information, influence perceptions, and decisively achieve mission objectives.
a. Active-duty CA NCOs are required to maintain control language proficiency in accordance with AR 11-6 Army Foreign Language Program, AR 614-200 Enlisted Assignments and Utilization Management, USASOC Regulation 350-1, and applicable unit policies. Soldiers scoring under the L3/R3 DLPT level or S3 OPI level must be recertified annually. Those who score L3/R3 on the DLPT or S3 on the OPI have a biennial (every 2-years) mandatory recertification requirement. Commanders must adhere to the policies identified in this chapter to re-evaluate their assigned ARSOF Soldiers’ proficiency IAW AR 11-6 dated 25 February 2022, Chapter 5-4. Army Special Operations Forces language proficiency testing.
b. Language capable ARSOF MOSs 38Z, 38R, 38W while serving as Drill Sergeants, Recruiters, and Observer Controllers are exempt from the 12-month language currency for 36- months while serving in these positions IAW Exception to Policy Memorandum dated 07 January 2020 signed by HQDAG-2 Language Team.
c. USAR members are encouraged to seek a language through the Defense Language Institute Foreign Language Center (DLIFLC) but are not required to do so in accordance with AR 11-6. Many USAR CA positions are specifically language coded based on the unit of assignment. Training may be authorized through the DLIFLC or numerous other approved programs and venues. USAR Soldiers that have received language training are required to attend 80 hours of consecutive language training or equivalent annually. All 38B Soldiers that meet minimum proficiency standards for any foreign language are authorized Foreign Language Proficiency Pay. 3 - 4. Military Bearing. Soldiers in all ranks must maintain a high level of physical, moral, and ethical discipline. NCOs in all grades should be well-versed in Army customs, courtesies, and traditions in accordance with: AR 600-25 (Salutes, Honors, and Courtesies), AR 670-1 (Wear and Appearance of Army Uniforms and Insignia), TC 3-21.5 (Drill and Ceremonies), TC 7-21.13 (Soldier’s Guide), TC 7-22.7 (The Noncommissioned Officer Guide), and applicable unit policies. 3 - 5. Required attributes. Soldiers wishing to obtain and maintain an Active Component ARSOF CA MOS (38Z, 38R or 38W) must demonstrate and internalize the following ARSOF core attributes:
a. Integrity. Be trustworthy and honest, act with honor and unwavering adherence to ethical standards.
b. Courage. Act on own convictions despite consequences, willing to sacrifice for a larger cause, be not paralyzed by fear of failure.
c. Perseverance. Work toward an end, have commitment, physical or mental resolve, motivated, give effort to the cause, do not quit.
d. Personal Responsibility. Be self-motivated and an autonomous self-starter, anticipate tasks and act, accordingly, take accountability for his/her actions.
e. Professionalism. Be a standard-bearer for the corps, have a professional image, to include a level of maturity and judgment mixed with confidence and humility, form sound opinions and make own decisions, stand behind his/her sensible decisions based on his/her experiences.
f. Adaptability. Personnel must demonstrate a high degree of adaptability and cognitive flexibility, maintaining composure while adjusting to dynamic environments. They are expected to employ creative problem-solving and think unconventionally to overcome challenges. A critical skill is the ability to recognize, understand, and navigate complex social networks within the human domain. Finally, CA professionals must exhibit the initiative to proactively shape the operational environment and set conditions to achieve desired outcomes.
g. Team Player. Demonstrates unwavering loyalty to the team and selfless dedication to the shared mission. Dependable, driven by a strong sense of duty to their fellow team members and the task at hand. By treating everyone with dignity and respect, they foster cohesion and recognize that the diverse strengths within the team are essential to mission success and serve as a force multiplier.
h. Capability. Maintain physical fitness, to include strength and agility, maintain operational knowledge, able to plan and communicate effectively. 3 - 6. Functional Training. Civil Affairs NCOs are highly encouraged to pursue formal military training opportunities that improve their survivability, tactical, and technical skills and increase their ability to lead Soldiers in any CA unit that they may be assigned. This includes but is not limited to all Additional Skill Identifier (ASI) and Special Qualifications Identifier (SQI) Producing Schools, Joint Service Courses, and International Military/Government Training.
a. Due to the high risk of isolation within potential denied areas inherent to the operational environment in which active-duty CATs operate, units are required to provide Survival, Evasion, Resistance, and Escape (SERE-C High Risk) for every active-duty CA Soldier IAW AR 525-28 para.2-21. Additionally, over 85% of Critical Leadership Development opportunities in the active component are airborne assignments, therefore all active-duty CA NCOs should successfully complete the Static Line Jumpmaster Course at the earliest opportunity.
b. USAR CA Forces support the conventional force with a structure that includes two Airborne BDEs and subordinate BNs and other Airborne coded positions. Individuals in these assignments must attend the Basic Airborne Course and maintain current jump status to be fully qualified for the position. Prior to assignment in a paid parachute position, non-airborne qualified TPU and AGR Soldiers should complete an Airborne Training Volunteer Statement. All NCOs in an airborne assignment should attend the Static Line Jump Master Course at the earliest opportunity. USAR Soldiers should also pursue job specific and functional courses that enhance professional technical skills and knowledge at various schools including the Army Reserve Readiness Training Center (ARRTC). Unless directed, USAR Soldiers do not have a SERE-C requirement. 3 - 7. Additional Military Educational Opportunities. Soldiers of all ranks are highly encouraged to continue their military training and education through self-paced and distributed-learning platforms. Courses in a wide variety of military topics can be found at the Army Training Information System (ATIS) Learning, Joint Knowledge Online (JKO), and Joint Special Operations University.', '9f27e9104f4abfa3cacf1a35e0d91ea660c165b1db6da47d986e994dd44426e8', 5, 7, null, 13, null, null, false, null),
  ('f3bf19ce-b5ae-525a-89fd-ac510df9adf5', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'development', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private-Specialist/Corporal — recommended professional military education (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', 'Decentralized promotion eligibility requirements for USAR Soldiers in the ranks of PVT, PV2, PFC, and SPC are outlined in AR 600-8-19, chapter 2.', '09c82084e48da7cd471be051b8dad473bc2e50406514bd85983510ff69ef7b46', 8, 8, null, 14, null, null, false, null),
  ('c35cdf99-5f9d-5b9a-9c95-460e9d1c78f4', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'development', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — recommended professional military education (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', 'Consideration by a local promotion board is mandatory for all Soldiers upon initially reaching primary zone eligibility.', 'e91be1813c1d62927b40eb321fe5f9af2912b38c4611e2ec983b85b0ec4eb022', 8, 8, null, 15, null, null, false, null),
  ('183322e9-bfa0-5a80-abff-f7c6bc994f7a', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'development', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — recommended professional military education (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', 'Active Component and AGR Staff Sergeants serving in a SOF unit should strive to complete the Joint Special Operations University Joint Fundamentals Course (CEP 1) and the JKO Enlisted Joint Professional Military Education Course (EJPME I). Staff Sergeants must take the initiative to become familiar with the Army Training Network and the Army Training Information System.', '8d3bfae76552c678c7759d9799d7802126efc414ab1fd29870f4d7333140bdab', 8, 8, null, 16, null, null, false, null),
  ('7a80ad37-4442-50e8-98d9-dd0b52be870c', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — recommended professional military education (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', 'Active Component and AGR Sergeants First Class serving in a SOF unit should strive to complete the Joint Special Operations University Enterprise Management Course (CEP 2) and the JKO Enlisted Joint Professional Military Education Course (EJPME II).', '48400fe1cf2e22ec7b333e457ea9ce1d8d6f5550c399124e5c151d8384d1ce84', 8, 8, null, 17, null, null, false, null),
  ('71dffcd1-5728-5a47-aae3-4b1c29a4d6d1', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant — recommended professional military education (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', 'Master Sergeants and First Sergeants should strive to complete the JKO Enlisted Joint Professional Military Education Course (EJPME II) if not already complete.', 'e35ac6c23beb48d51ba6f0074a509453cb0dc9bfbfd8cf634cd1d70a107f780c', 8, 8, null, 18, null, null, false, null),
  ('c41891b2-a60c-57a5-bc35-76accf5ae28b', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'development', null, null, 'General guidance (broadening billets, competitive boards, education resources, professional reading)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '3 - 8. Broadening billets and assignments. This covers a wide range of positions and duties outside of one’s MOS. CA Enlisted and NCO assignments are classified into three basic categories: Critical Leadership Developmental (CD), Developmental, and Broadening. All assignments are developmental.
a. Critical Leadership assignments are vital for professional development, providing a foundational understanding of CA operations while serving as the framework to assess a Soldier''s potential for career advancement.
b. Developmental assignments are those assignments that enhance warfighting capability but are not deemed so fundamental to CA Soldiers or NCOs progression that they qualify as a Critical Leadership assignment.
c. Broadening assignments are those assignments that provide a purposeful expansion of knowledge and capability and are fundamental to the continued growth throughout the CA Soldiers or NCOs career lifecycle. For any position to be considered broadening, it must either be an operational or institutional position in an organization at the operational or strategic levels providing an experience above and beyond the tactical level or an MOS-immaterial position that increases skill in Army policy and programs outside of their CMF. Assignments and billets include, but are not limited to, assignments at a Theater Special Operations Command TSOC, GCC, USASOC, 1st SFC (A), Multi-Domain Effects Battalion (MDEB), Multi-Domain Task Force (MDTF), Theater Information Advantage Detachment (TIAD), Division or CORP G-9, SFG S-9, OSC or positions such as Observer Controller/Trainer (JRTC/NTC), Drill Sergeant, Recruiter (including SORB), Inspector General (IG) NCO, Brigade level Sexual Assault Response Coordinator or Sexual Harassment/Assault Response and Prevention Coordinator, and EOA assignments. Broadening often occurs following Critical Leadership assignments. The full list of assignments can be found in the paragraphs below for each grade plate. To be considered ''Best Qualified'' and competitive for future promotions, CA Soldiers must complete their Critical Leadership assignment, and all required professional military education at each grade. 3 - 9. Competitive Boards. Soldiers at all ranks are encouraged to surpass standards in presence, intellect, leadership, development, and achievement, setting the example for their peers and subordinates through courage, confidence, competence, military bearing, general military knowledge, and warrior skills. Those seeking to challenge themselves and excel beyond their peers should consider competing in events such as: Active Component Best Civil Affairs Competition, U.S. Army Best Medic Competition, U.S. Army Best Ranger Competition, U.S. Army Best Sapper Competition, U.S. Army Best Warrior/Best Squad Competition, Soldier and NCO of the Month, Quarter, and Year boards. NCOs at all ranks are encouraged to seek acceptance into the prestigious Sergeant Audie Murphy or Sergeant Morales Clubs. 3 - 10. Credentials/Certifications. Must maintain a current, valid, unrestricted National Registry Emergency Medical Technician Certification and Advanced Tactical Paramedic Certification to retain MOS 38W or W4 ASI. 3 - 11. Civilian Education. Civilian education is a vital component of self-development for Civil Affairs Soldiers. Prioritizing education early in your career accelerates professional growth and enhances flexibility in navigating operational demands. Although education is an important factor enhancing potential value to the Army, comparison of SMs based on civilian education attainments should be viewed as an indicator of individual initiative rather than as a prerequisite for selection as most/highly qualified. College is favorable to set SMs apart from their peers, but it is not a requirement for promotion. Soldiers should pursue degree programs relevant to their MOS to maximize credit for prior military training. Explore the Service-member Opportunity Colleges Army Degree (SOCAD) website for more information, https://www.education.army.mil/socad.aspx. For details on obtaining college credits, refer to paragraph 10.
a. Staff Sergeants should aim to complete an associate degree, or at a minimum complete 60 semester hours. Staff Sergeants that have already completed a bachelor’s degree are highly competitive to pursue a master’s degree with. Civil Affairs Medical Sergeants (38W) students are enrolled into the Uniformed Services University (USU) College of Allied Health Sciences (CAHS) upon admittance to the Special Operation Medical Sergeants Course. Upon graduation of the Special Operations Combat Medical Course, a medic is awarded the Associates of Science (AS) in Health Science from the Uniformed Services University (USU). The primary location of training within USASOC is the Joint Special Operations Medical Training Center.
b. Sergeants First Class should have already completed an associate degree or 90 semester hours by 13 years TIS and should have had the opportunity to work towards or complete their bachelor’s degree. Sergeants First Class who have already completed a bachelor’s degree are highly encouraged to pursue a master’s degree with National Defense University (NDU).
c. Proponent note. National Defense University (NDU). Selected NCOs attend the highly competitive Joint Special Operations Master of Arts program conducted over ten months at Fort Bragg, NC, producing a Master of Arts in Strategic Security Studies. This education better prepares CA NCOs for assignments in Army Educational Requirements System (AERS) coded “98” billets. The gaining command has the responsibility to ensure NCOs assigned against validated AERS positions are properly utilized. Per the regulation (AR 621-1 para 4-5a). Graduates of the program have enhanced strategic situational awareness and are better prepared for the complexities of special operations in interagency and multinational contexts. The application for NDU requires endorsement by the first O-6 in the officer’s chain of command. Graduates of this venue incur a one to two ratio utilization duration tour per AR 621-1 and AR 350-100.
d. Proponent note. NCOs who want to attend must have completed appropriate Noncommissioned Officer educational programs for grade and years of service. NCOs normally must have no less than 6 years-time in service or no more than 13 years-time in service (TIS) as of the report date to the academic institution. NCOs with more than 13-year TIS (waiver possible) will be considered on a case-by-case basis. Must be indefinite or have sufficient enlistment remaining equal to or greater than three times the length of requested schooling. Must be in the grade of E7 or above.
e. Master Sergeants and First Sergeants should have at least 120 semester hours of civilian college education towards a bachelor or higher-level degree. There are certain positions or billets that may require a master’s degree to apply. Master Sergeants and First Sergeants should complete a bachelor’s degree and be willing to work towards a master’s degree while serving in the assigned billet.
f. Sergeants Major and Command Sergeants Major should have a bachelor’s degree and are highly encouraged to obtain a master’s degree. Joint Special Operations University courses, especially CEP 4 (Enlisted Academy Summit course) will become available if selected to serve at the nominative level. Due to limited authorizations and fiercely competitive records, civilian education may be considered a major factor for selection to Command Sergeant Major (CSM). There are certain positions or billets that may require a master’s degree to apply. At a minimum, Sergeants Major must have completed a bachelor’s degree and be willing to work towards a master’s degree while serving in a AERS 98 coded billet. 3 - 12. College Opportunities. Many opportunities and resources exist to support CA Soldiers in pursuing their civilian education: Army Continuing Education System, Army University, Defense Activity for Non-Traditional Education Support, Army IgnitED, Joint Service Transcript, and Advanced Civil Schooling opportunities. Achievement of college or higher-level degree credits can also be completed during the MOS training process, as modeled by the 38W SOCM program degree.
a. Army Continuing Education System (ACES). ACES programs and services support the self- development domain of the Army leader development tenets by providing Soldiers opportunities for traditional and technology-based education and support for personal and professional career goals. The ACES programs assist Soldiers in achieving the lifelong synthesis of the knowledge, skills, and experiences required for success. Enlisted Soldiers should master the academic skills needed to perform the duties of their primary military occupational specialty and to meet prerequisites for the Noncommissioned Officer Professional Development System (NCOPDS) in accordance with AR 621-5.
b. The DOD MWR Libraries virtual collection includes e-books, audiobooks, academic support, test prep, professional development, and a vast array of learning resources and online content. This platform provides free access to resources including but not limited to, Mango Languages, Universal Calss, EBSCO Learning Express, Gale OneFile: Military and Intelligence, and World Book Student. You can access these materials by going to https://army.dodmwrlibraries.org and logging in with your DoWID number and birth date.
c. Army University. The Army University is a professional military education university system of the United States Army. It is the most extensive professional military education system globally with over 150,000 soldiers educated in more than 88 occupations across its worldwide network of 70 schools. https://armyuniversity.edu/.
d. Defense Activity for Non-Traditional Education Support. Soldiers are encouraged to earn additional college credit for knowledge acquired outside of the traditional classroom through the Defense Activity for Non-Traditional Education Support (DANTES) https://www.dantes.mil/. College Level Examination Program (CLEP) and DANTES Subject Standardized Tests (DSST) are exams that test the mastery of college-level subjects acquired a variety of ways–through general academic instructions, significant independent study, or extracurricular work.
e. Army IgnitED. Army IgnitED is a virtual gateway for all eligible Soldiers to request college tuition assistance or funding for professional credentials and certificates online for classroom and distance learning. It allows Soldiers to manage their education records including college classes, testing, and Army Education Counselor support. Through Online programs, Education Centers and On-site programs offer over 300,000+ college courses. Credentialing Opportunity Online Learning (COOL). COOL helps Army service members find information on certifications and licenses related to their military occupation and civilian career. This assists in the operational aspect when working with unified action partners. There are 35-line areas of certifications for the CA NCO that are applicable to their skills. https://www.cool.osd.mil/army/index.html.
f. Joint Service Transcript. Joint Service Transcript is an academically accepted document approved by the American Council on Education (ACE) to validate a service member''s military occupational experience and training along with the corresponding ACE college credit recommendations. Joint Service Transcript can be accessed at https://jst.doded.mil/jst/home.
g. Advanced Civil Schooling. Advanced Civil Schooling opportunities are available for active component CA NCOs. The National Defense University (NDU) Joint Special Operations Master of Arts Strategic Security Studies degree program is conducted under the auspices of the U.S. Army John F. Kennedy Special Warfare Center and School (USAJFKSWCS) provides a Master of Science. 38Zs with a minimum of six years’ Time in Service (TIS) and no more than 13 years TIS are eligible. Application requirements are published annually.
h. Proponent note. NCOs who received an advanced degree through an Army civilian schooling program are required to serve in an initial AERS validated position for a minimum of 24 months per AR 621-1 MAY 2024. 3 - 13. Professional Reading. Civil Affairs Soldiers of all ranks should pursue self-development and understanding of the Army profession through professional reading. The Sergeant Major of the Army reading list, published by the Association of the United States Army NCO reading list broken down by rank: https://www.ausa.org/nco-reading-list and https://www.ausa.org/nco- soldier-programs. 3 - 14. Guided Self-development by rank', 'eb49f1cc003e37b7eddf256d198e6d91c7707b2393f357bd6b6a21ce9e845f81', 8, 11, null, 19, null, null, false, null),
  ('daafd75d-8f2d-5215-b969-485e66936495', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'development', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private-Specialist/Corporal (USAR only) — recommended publications and reading (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '(1) All Skill Level 1 CA Specialists should focus on gaining familiarization with Army and CA CMF terminology, and operations at the company and team level. Soldiers should study and master the following military publications: TC 7–21.13 (Soldier’s Guide), STP 21–1–SMCT (Soldier’s Manual of Common Task, SL1), FM 7–22 (Holistic Health and Fitness (H2F)), TC 4- 02.1 (First Aid), AR 670–1 (Wear and Appearance of Army Uniforms and Insignia), TM 9-1005- 319-10 (Operators Manual for Carbine 5.56mm, M4A1), TM 9-1005 - 470-10 (Operators Manual for MHS: M17, M18 Pistol), FM 3-25.26 (Map Reading and Land Navigation), all -10 level technical manuals associated with their equipment, and battle drills associated with their current assignment.
(2) Recommended Reading: Small Unit Leadership: A Commonsense Approach by Dandridge Malone, A Bell for Adano by John Hersey, Ender’s Game by Orson Scott Card, How to Win Friends and Influence People by Dale Carnegie.', 'dabeef07d046d397ae729b0b91136efe155308dec8455cc7ff33a5f56d1f167b', 12, 12, null, 20, null, null, false, null),
  ('41639105-3198-5b4d-8f90-db0969b0e045', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'development', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant (USAR only) — recommended publications and reading (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '(1) Foundational: TC 7-22.7 (Noncommissioned Officer Guide), STP 21-24-SMCT (Warrior Tasks, SL 2-4), FM 3-57 (Civil Affairs Operations), ADP 1 (The Army), ADP 3-07 (Stability), AR 623-3 (Evaluation Reporting System), ADP 6-22 (Army Leadership and the Profession), AR 600-9 (The Army Body Composition Program), DA PAM 600-25 (base document and Civil Affairs Chapter), ADP 7-0 (Training) and ADP 6-0 (Mission Command).
(2) Recommended Reading: The Ugly American, Starship Troopers, Influence: The Psychology of Persuasion, Savage Wars of Peace.', '2c40a8fba57146b5a23004c27c981ddb74d179e4df956979c191f3e7b2ff2cfd', 12, 12, null, 21, null, null, false, null),
  ('3366fca2-6136-5b2b-b527-46f81f5227ad', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'development', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — recommended publications and reading (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '(1) Builds Upon Sergeant: JP 3-57 (Civil-Military Operations), FM 1-02.2 (Military Symbols), FM 1-02.1 (Operational Terms), ADP 3-0 (Operations), ADP 3-05 (Army Special Operations), ADP 3-37 (Protection), ADP 7-0 (Training), FM 3-98 (Reconnaissance and Security Operations), FM 3-90-2 (Reconnaissance, Security, and Tactical Enabling Tasks Volume 2), ATP 6-22.01 (The Counseling Process), ATP 3-50.20 (SERE Planning and Preparation), ADP 5-0 (The Operations Process) for early exposure to operational planning.
(2) Recommended Reading: Talking to Strangers, Inside a U.S. Embassy: Diplomacy at Work, Leaders Eat Last, David and Goliath, The Road Not Taken, The Undoing Project, Team of Teams, This Kind of War.', '3ca98f844ed6c131a7398b20da528819a9fd3a15f612cdce15f609539a6508ad', 12, 12, null, 22, null, null, false, null),
  ('44ffa55b-f764-5bf2-886b-2905b9ce37a8', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — recommended publications and reading (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '(1) Builds Upon Staff Sergeant: FM 6-0 (Commander and Staff Organization and Operations) or ADP 6-0 (Mission Command), FM 3-0 (Operations), ADP 5-0 (The Operations Process), ATP 5- 19 (Risk Management), ATP 3-60 (Targeting), FM 7-22.7 (The Noncommissioned Officer Guide), TC 3-57.51 (Civil Preparation of the Battlefield), FM 7-0 (Training), FM 7-22 (Holistic Health and Fitness), FM 6-22 (Developing Leaders), FM 3-13 (Information Operations), FM 3-55 (Information Collection), ATP 2-01.3 (Intelligence Preparation of the Battlefield), AR 25-50 (Preparing and Managing Correspondence), ADP 1-01 (Doctrine Primer), ADP 2-0 (Intelligence), ADP 3-90 (Offense and Defense), ADP 4-0 (Sustainment), ATP 3-57.X series, AR 600-8-22 (Military Awards) and AR 27-10 (Military Justice).
(2) Recommended Reading: Start with Why, Outliers, The Black Swan, World Order, The Training Ground, The Third Pillar, The Generals, The History of the Peloponnesian War, The 4 Disciplines of Execution, The Prince.', 'a90cebeda2e7df3171fb47ce8ae18091fa86164b0d278421cceb69dfd6db3b1b', 12, 13, null, 23, null, null, false, null),
  ('aef2afee-d010-56bb-9fef-18771f78fa7f', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant and First Sergeant — recommended publications and reading (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '(1) Builds Upon Sergeant First Class: AR 600-20 (Army Command Policy), FM 7-22 (Army Physical Readiness Training), AR 11-6 (Army Foreign Language Program), AR 15-185 (Army Board for Correction of Military Records), AR 220-1 (Army Unit Status Reporting), AR 601-280 (Army Retention Program), AR 614-200 (Enlisted Assignments and Utilization), Mastery of Army Training Network.
(2) Recommended Reading: Winning, Becoming a Resonant Leader, Mindset, Good to Great.', '399a821dc9bf14796808069fedef8c341712274cef656255af4d7eaeaa67718a', 13, 13, null, 24, null, null, false, null),
  ('484ef014-06b4-543d-80c7-005726bdf4c5', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'development', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major and Command Sergeant Major — recommended publications and reading (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '(1) Sergeants Major and Command Sergeants Major should continue to study and master regulations, policies, and doctrine as updates are published.
(2) Recommended professional reading for the CA Sergeant Major is: Once an Eagle by Anton Myrer, Thinking Fast and Slow by Daniel Kahneman, The Slight Edge: Turning Simple Disciplines into Massive Success and Happiness by Jeff Olson and John David Mann, Nudge: Improving Decisions About Health, Wealth, and Happiness by Richard M. Thaler and Cass R Sunstein.', '06cf8a73620c0a42a287bb612674d6c85280d3b701829ae4c4ea8be0867d4ceb', 13, 13, null, 25, null, null, false, null),
  ('c1a523eb-e4cb-55c6-bfb3-35236ce91313', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'development', null, null, 'Overview', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '3 - 1. Civil Affairs Soldiers are expected to succeed in missions that are often demanding and high-risk within an increasingly complex and dynamic contemporary operating environment. Therefore, the CA branch naturally produces a high concentration of highly skilled NCOs. NCOs that consistently exceed standards and demonstrate mental and physical capabilities above their peers are more competitive for demanding positions and career advancement. The Special Operations Center of Excellence, the CA Proponent, the United States Army Human Resources Command (HRC), and the NCO Professional Development System (NCOPDS) all play an important part in the career development of the force; however, each Soldier is ultimately the true steward of their career. To assist leaders with talent management, NCOs at every level must seek opportunities to demonstrate their leadership potential and distinguish themselves from their peers. Refer to DA PAM 600-25 (U.S. Army Noncommissioned Officer Professional Development Guide), para 2-15. (https://www.army.mil/g-1#org-g-1-publications) for more on Self-development. 3 - 2. Physical Fitness. The ability to conduct CAO across the competition continuum requires CA Soldiers to be physically capable of operating in the most rigorous environments. All personnel must maintain a high state of readiness. Physical fitness contributes to NCOs overall wellness and ability to set the example for others. A holistic health and fitness (H2F) approach is essential to readiness when assessing a Soldier’s physical and mental status. Additionally, it assists to design programs to sustain or improve that Soldier’s ability to meet the demands of their MOSs, duty assignments, and combat-specific tasks. While it is a command responsibility to establish physical training policies and allocate sufficient resources for Soldiers to meet Army and unit standards and goals, it is an individual responsibility to meet those goals. Soldiers must keep themselves in a high state of physical readiness. All Soldiers are encouraged to use existing resources to assist them in making a healthy lifestyle, good dietary choices, and developing an individual goal-oriented physical training regimen. CA Soldiers will meet all standards for their respective grades as specified in DA PAM 611-21. NCOs assigned to either the Active Component or Reserve Components must meet all physical fitness requirements as directed by their command to ensure maximum mission readiness to fulfill global requirements with Joint Special Operations Forces (SOF) partners and support Joint Commanders. 3 - 3. Language: Your Strategic Advantage.
(1) Fluency equals operational success: As a Civil Affairs NCO in a language-capable CMF, your language proficiency is mission-critical. Achieve and maintain the highest level of fluency possible for your assigned control language (CLANG).
(2) Cultural Expertise Amplifies Impact: Combine your language skills with a deep understanding of regional cultures, customs, values, and social norms. This enables you to connect meaningfully, build trust, and navigate complex situations.
(3) Stay Current, Stay Relevant: The world is constantly evolving. Continuously update your knowledge of regional history, current events, and emerging trends to anticipate challenges and capitalize on opportunities.
(4) Communication is Your Weapon: Leverage your language and cultural expertise to engage local populations, gather critical information, influence perceptions, and decisively achieve mission objectives.
a. Active-duty CA NCOs are required to maintain control language proficiency in accordance with AR 11-6 Army Foreign Language Program, AR 614-200 Enlisted Assignments and Utilization Management, USASOC Regulation 350-1, and applicable unit policies. Soldiers scoring under the L3/R3 DLPT level or S3 OPI level must be recertified annually. Those who score L3/R3 on the DLPT or S3 on the OPI have a biennial (every 2-years) mandatory recertification requirement. Commanders must adhere to the policies identified in this chapter to re-evaluate their assigned ARSOF Soldiers’ proficiency IAW AR 11-6 dated 25 February 2022, Chapter 5-4. Army Special Operations Forces language proficiency testing.
b. Language capable ARSOF MOSs 38Z, 38R, 38W while serving as Drill Sergeants, Recruiters, and Observer Controllers are exempt from the 12-month language currency for 36- months while serving in these positions IAW Exception to Policy Memorandum dated 07 January 2020 signed by HQDAG-2 Language Team.
c. USAR members are encouraged to seek a language through the Defense Language Institute Foreign Language Center (DLIFLC) but are not required to do so in accordance with AR 11-6. Many USAR CA positions are specifically language coded based on the unit of assignment. Training may be authorized through the DLIFLC or numerous other approved programs and venues. USAR Soldiers that have received language training are required to attend 80 hours of consecutive language training or equivalent annually. All 38B Soldiers that meet minimum proficiency standards for any foreign language are authorized Foreign Language Proficiency Pay. 3 - 4. Military Bearing. Soldiers in all ranks must maintain a high level of physical, moral, and ethical discipline. NCOs in all grades should be well-versed in Army customs, courtesies, and traditions in accordance with: AR 600-25 (Salutes, Honors, and Courtesies), AR 670-1 (Wear and Appearance of Army Uniforms and Insignia), TC 3-21.5 (Drill and Ceremonies), TC 7-21.13 (Soldier’s Guide), TC 7-22.7 (The Noncommissioned Officer Guide), and applicable unit policies. 3 - 5. Required attributes. Soldiers wishing to obtain and maintain an Active Component ARSOF CA MOS (38Z, 38R or 38W) must demonstrate and internalize the following ARSOF core attributes:
a. Integrity. Be trustworthy and honest, act with honor and unwavering adherence to ethical standards.
b. Courage. Act on own convictions despite consequences, willing to sacrifice for a larger cause, be not paralyzed by fear of failure.
c. Perseverance. Work toward an end, have commitment, physical or mental resolve, motivated, give effort to the cause, do not quit.
d. Personal Responsibility. Be self-motivated and an autonomous self-starter, anticipate tasks and act, accordingly, take accountability for his/her actions.
e. Professionalism. Be a standard-bearer for the corps, have a professional image, to include a level of maturity and judgment mixed with confidence and humility, form sound opinions and make own decisions, stand behind his/her sensible decisions based on his/her experiences.
f. Adaptability. Personnel must demonstrate a high degree of adaptability and cognitive flexibility, maintaining composure while adjusting to dynamic environments. They are expected to employ creative problem-solving and think unconventionally to overcome challenges. A critical skill is the ability to recognize, understand, and navigate complex social networks within the human domain. Finally, CA professionals must exhibit the initiative to proactively shape the operational environment and set conditions to achieve desired outcomes.
g. Team Player. Demonstrates unwavering loyalty to the team and selfless dedication to the shared mission. Dependable, driven by a strong sense of duty to their fellow team members and the task at hand. By treating everyone with dignity and respect, they foster cohesion and recognize that the diverse strengths within the team are essential to mission success and serve as a force multiplier.
h. Capability. Maintain physical fitness, to include strength and agility, maintain operational knowledge, able to plan and communicate effectively. 3 - 6. Functional Training. Civil Affairs NCOs are highly encouraged to pursue formal military training opportunities that improve their survivability, tactical, and technical skills and increase their ability to lead Soldiers in any CA unit that they may be assigned. This includes but is not limited to all Additional Skill Identifier (ASI) and Special Qualifications Identifier (SQI) Producing Schools, Joint Service Courses, and International Military/Government Training.
a. Due to the high risk of isolation within potential denied areas inherent to the operational environment in which active-duty CATs operate, units are required to provide Survival, Evasion, Resistance, and Escape (SERE-C High Risk) for every active-duty CA Soldier IAW AR 525-28 para.2-21. Additionally, over 85% of Critical Leadership Development opportunities in the active component are airborne assignments, therefore all active-duty CA NCOs should successfully complete the Static Line Jumpmaster Course at the earliest opportunity.
b. USAR CA Forces support the conventional force with a structure that includes two Airborne BDEs and subordinate BNs and other Airborne coded positions. Individuals in these assignments must attend the Basic Airborne Course and maintain current jump status to be fully qualified for the position. Prior to assignment in a paid parachute position, non-airborne qualified TPU and AGR Soldiers should complete an Airborne Training Volunteer Statement. All NCOs in an airborne assignment should attend the Static Line Jump Master Course at the earliest opportunity. USAR Soldiers should also pursue job specific and functional courses that enhance professional technical skills and knowledge at various schools including the Army Reserve Readiness Training Center (ARRTC). Unless directed, USAR Soldiers do not have a SERE-C requirement. 3 - 7. Additional Military Educational Opportunities. Soldiers of all ranks are highly encouraged to continue their military training and education through self-paced and distributed-learning platforms. Courses in a wide variety of military topics can be found at the Army Training Information System (ATIS) Learning, Joint Knowledge Online (JKO), and Joint Special Operations University.', '9f27e9104f4abfa3cacf1a35e0d91ea660c165b1db6da47d986e994dd44426e8', 5, 7, null, 26, null, null, false, null),
  ('1439d43f-a7fc-5280-aac7-c6d0d286b6c1', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'development', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private-Specialist/Corporal — recommended professional military education (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', 'Decentralized promotion eligibility requirements for USAR Soldiers in the ranks of PVT, PV2, PFC, and SPC are outlined in AR 600-8-19, chapter 2.', '09c82084e48da7cd471be051b8dad473bc2e50406514bd85983510ff69ef7b46', 8, 8, null, 27, null, null, false, null),
  ('073a313e-41da-5ea0-98a1-589e8a3957fb', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'development', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — recommended professional military education (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', 'Consideration by a local promotion board is mandatory for all Soldiers upon initially reaching primary zone eligibility.', 'e91be1813c1d62927b40eb321fe5f9af2912b38c4611e2ec983b85b0ec4eb022', 8, 8, null, 28, null, null, false, null),
  ('26a615a4-ba79-59a5-9192-a01c6bec6b3c', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'development', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — recommended professional military education (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', 'Active Component and AGR Staff Sergeants serving in a SOF unit should strive to complete the Joint Special Operations University Joint Fundamentals Course (CEP 1) and the JKO Enlisted Joint Professional Military Education Course (EJPME I). Staff Sergeants must take the initiative to become familiar with the Army Training Network and the Army Training Information System.', '8d3bfae76552c678c7759d9799d7802126efc414ab1fd29870f4d7333140bdab', 8, 8, null, 29, null, null, false, null),
  ('bc226cf3-f6c0-55a2-bd75-9803cd0d063b', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — recommended professional military education (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', 'Active Component and AGR Sergeants First Class serving in a SOF unit should strive to complete the Joint Special Operations University Enterprise Management Course (CEP 2) and the JKO Enlisted Joint Professional Military Education Course (EJPME II).', '48400fe1cf2e22ec7b333e457ea9ce1d8d6f5550c399124e5c151d8384d1ce84', 8, 8, null, 30, null, null, false, null),
  ('5b5c6a92-88de-5797-a3f0-f4c0c165160c', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant — recommended professional military education (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', 'Master Sergeants and First Sergeants should strive to complete the JKO Enlisted Joint Professional Military Education Course (EJPME II) if not already complete.', 'e35ac6c23beb48d51ba6f0074a509453cb0dc9bfbfd8cf634cd1d70a107f780c', 8, 8, null, 31, null, null, false, null),
  ('de3a61eb-476a-550c-8cf9-d675af69472f', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'development', null, null, 'General guidance (broadening billets, competitive boards, education resources, professional reading)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '3 - 8. Broadening billets and assignments. This covers a wide range of positions and duties outside of one’s MOS. CA Enlisted and NCO assignments are classified into three basic categories: Critical Leadership Developmental (CD), Developmental, and Broadening. All assignments are developmental.
a. Critical Leadership assignments are vital for professional development, providing a foundational understanding of CA operations while serving as the framework to assess a Soldier''s potential for career advancement.
b. Developmental assignments are those assignments that enhance warfighting capability but are not deemed so fundamental to CA Soldiers or NCOs progression that they qualify as a Critical Leadership assignment.
c. Broadening assignments are those assignments that provide a purposeful expansion of knowledge and capability and are fundamental to the continued growth throughout the CA Soldiers or NCOs career lifecycle. For any position to be considered broadening, it must either be an operational or institutional position in an organization at the operational or strategic levels providing an experience above and beyond the tactical level or an MOS-immaterial position that increases skill in Army policy and programs outside of their CMF. Assignments and billets include, but are not limited to, assignments at a Theater Special Operations Command TSOC, GCC, USASOC, 1st SFC (A), Multi-Domain Effects Battalion (MDEB), Multi-Domain Task Force (MDTF), Theater Information Advantage Detachment (TIAD), Division or CORP G-9, SFG S-9, OSC or positions such as Observer Controller/Trainer (JRTC/NTC), Drill Sergeant, Recruiter (including SORB), Inspector General (IG) NCO, Brigade level Sexual Assault Response Coordinator or Sexual Harassment/Assault Response and Prevention Coordinator, and EOA assignments. Broadening often occurs following Critical Leadership assignments. The full list of assignments can be found in the paragraphs below for each grade plate. To be considered ''Best Qualified'' and competitive for future promotions, CA Soldiers must complete their Critical Leadership assignment, and all required professional military education at each grade. 3 - 9. Competitive Boards. Soldiers at all ranks are encouraged to surpass standards in presence, intellect, leadership, development, and achievement, setting the example for their peers and subordinates through courage, confidence, competence, military bearing, general military knowledge, and warrior skills. Those seeking to challenge themselves and excel beyond their peers should consider competing in events such as: Active Component Best Civil Affairs Competition, U.S. Army Best Medic Competition, U.S. Army Best Ranger Competition, U.S. Army Best Sapper Competition, U.S. Army Best Warrior/Best Squad Competition, Soldier and NCO of the Month, Quarter, and Year boards. NCOs at all ranks are encouraged to seek acceptance into the prestigious Sergeant Audie Murphy or Sergeant Morales Clubs. 3 - 10. Credentials/Certifications. Must maintain a current, valid, unrestricted National Registry Emergency Medical Technician Certification and Advanced Tactical Paramedic Certification to retain MOS 38W or W4 ASI. 3 - 11. Civilian Education. Civilian education is a vital component of self-development for Civil Affairs Soldiers. Prioritizing education early in your career accelerates professional growth and enhances flexibility in navigating operational demands. Although education is an important factor enhancing potential value to the Army, comparison of SMs based on civilian education attainments should be viewed as an indicator of individual initiative rather than as a prerequisite for selection as most/highly qualified. College is favorable to set SMs apart from their peers, but it is not a requirement for promotion. Soldiers should pursue degree programs relevant to their MOS to maximize credit for prior military training. Explore the Service-member Opportunity Colleges Army Degree (SOCAD) website for more information, https://www.education.army.mil/socad.aspx. For details on obtaining college credits, refer to paragraph 10.
a. Staff Sergeants should aim to complete an associate degree, or at a minimum complete 60 semester hours. Staff Sergeants that have already completed a bachelor’s degree are highly competitive to pursue a master’s degree with. Civil Affairs Medical Sergeants (38W) students are enrolled into the Uniformed Services University (USU) College of Allied Health Sciences (CAHS) upon admittance to the Special Operation Medical Sergeants Course. Upon graduation of the Special Operations Combat Medical Course, a medic is awarded the Associates of Science (AS) in Health Science from the Uniformed Services University (USU). The primary location of training within USASOC is the Joint Special Operations Medical Training Center.
b. Sergeants First Class should have already completed an associate degree or 90 semester hours by 13 years TIS and should have had the opportunity to work towards or complete their bachelor’s degree. Sergeants First Class who have already completed a bachelor’s degree are highly encouraged to pursue a master’s degree with National Defense University (NDU).
c. Proponent note. National Defense University (NDU). Selected NCOs attend the highly competitive Joint Special Operations Master of Arts program conducted over ten months at Fort Bragg, NC, producing a Master of Arts in Strategic Security Studies. This education better prepares CA NCOs for assignments in Army Educational Requirements System (AERS) coded “98” billets. The gaining command has the responsibility to ensure NCOs assigned against validated AERS positions are properly utilized. Per the regulation (AR 621-1 para 4-5a). Graduates of the program have enhanced strategic situational awareness and are better prepared for the complexities of special operations in interagency and multinational contexts. The application for NDU requires endorsement by the first O-6 in the officer’s chain of command. Graduates of this venue incur a one to two ratio utilization duration tour per AR 621-1 and AR 350-100.
d. Proponent note. NCOs who want to attend must have completed appropriate Noncommissioned Officer educational programs for grade and years of service. NCOs normally must have no less than 6 years-time in service or no more than 13 years-time in service (TIS) as of the report date to the academic institution. NCOs with more than 13-year TIS (waiver possible) will be considered on a case-by-case basis. Must be indefinite or have sufficient enlistment remaining equal to or greater than three times the length of requested schooling. Must be in the grade of E7 or above.
e. Master Sergeants and First Sergeants should have at least 120 semester hours of civilian college education towards a bachelor or higher-level degree. There are certain positions or billets that may require a master’s degree to apply. Master Sergeants and First Sergeants should complete a bachelor’s degree and be willing to work towards a master’s degree while serving in the assigned billet.
f. Sergeants Major and Command Sergeants Major should have a bachelor’s degree and are highly encouraged to obtain a master’s degree. Joint Special Operations University courses, especially CEP 4 (Enlisted Academy Summit course) will become available if selected to serve at the nominative level. Due to limited authorizations and fiercely competitive records, civilian education may be considered a major factor for selection to Command Sergeant Major (CSM). There are certain positions or billets that may require a master’s degree to apply. At a minimum, Sergeants Major must have completed a bachelor’s degree and be willing to work towards a master’s degree while serving in a AERS 98 coded billet. 3 - 12. College Opportunities. Many opportunities and resources exist to support CA Soldiers in pursuing their civilian education: Army Continuing Education System, Army University, Defense Activity for Non-Traditional Education Support, Army IgnitED, Joint Service Transcript, and Advanced Civil Schooling opportunities. Achievement of college or higher-level degree credits can also be completed during the MOS training process, as modeled by the 38W SOCM program degree.
a. Army Continuing Education System (ACES). ACES programs and services support the self- development domain of the Army leader development tenets by providing Soldiers opportunities for traditional and technology-based education and support for personal and professional career goals. The ACES programs assist Soldiers in achieving the lifelong synthesis of the knowledge, skills, and experiences required for success. Enlisted Soldiers should master the academic skills needed to perform the duties of their primary military occupational specialty and to meet prerequisites for the Noncommissioned Officer Professional Development System (NCOPDS) in accordance with AR 621-5.
b. The DOD MWR Libraries virtual collection includes e-books, audiobooks, academic support, test prep, professional development, and a vast array of learning resources and online content. This platform provides free access to resources including but not limited to, Mango Languages, Universal Calss, EBSCO Learning Express, Gale OneFile: Military and Intelligence, and World Book Student. You can access these materials by going to https://army.dodmwrlibraries.org and logging in with your DoWID number and birth date.
c. Army University. The Army University is a professional military education university system of the United States Army. It is the most extensive professional military education system globally with over 150,000 soldiers educated in more than 88 occupations across its worldwide network of 70 schools. https://armyuniversity.edu/.
d. Defense Activity for Non-Traditional Education Support. Soldiers are encouraged to earn additional college credit for knowledge acquired outside of the traditional classroom through the Defense Activity for Non-Traditional Education Support (DANTES) https://www.dantes.mil/. College Level Examination Program (CLEP) and DANTES Subject Standardized Tests (DSST) are exams that test the mastery of college-level subjects acquired a variety of ways–through general academic instructions, significant independent study, or extracurricular work.
e. Army IgnitED. Army IgnitED is a virtual gateway for all eligible Soldiers to request college tuition assistance or funding for professional credentials and certificates online for classroom and distance learning. It allows Soldiers to manage their education records including college classes, testing, and Army Education Counselor support. Through Online programs, Education Centers and On-site programs offer over 300,000+ college courses. Credentialing Opportunity Online Learning (COOL). COOL helps Army service members find information on certifications and licenses related to their military occupation and civilian career. This assists in the operational aspect when working with unified action partners. There are 35-line areas of certifications for the CA NCO that are applicable to their skills. https://www.cool.osd.mil/army/index.html.
f. Joint Service Transcript. Joint Service Transcript is an academically accepted document approved by the American Council on Education (ACE) to validate a service member''s military occupational experience and training along with the corresponding ACE college credit recommendations. Joint Service Transcript can be accessed at https://jst.doded.mil/jst/home.
g. Advanced Civil Schooling. Advanced Civil Schooling opportunities are available for active component CA NCOs. The National Defense University (NDU) Joint Special Operations Master of Arts Strategic Security Studies degree program is conducted under the auspices of the U.S. Army John F. Kennedy Special Warfare Center and School (USAJFKSWCS) provides a Master of Science. 38Zs with a minimum of six years’ Time in Service (TIS) and no more than 13 years TIS are eligible. Application requirements are published annually.
h. Proponent note. NCOs who received an advanced degree through an Army civilian schooling program are required to serve in an initial AERS validated position for a minimum of 24 months per AR 621-1 MAY 2024. 3 - 13. Professional Reading. Civil Affairs Soldiers of all ranks should pursue self-development and understanding of the Army profession through professional reading. The Sergeant Major of the Army reading list, published by the Association of the United States Army NCO reading list broken down by rank: https://www.ausa.org/nco-reading-list and https://www.ausa.org/nco- soldier-programs. 3 - 14. Guided Self-development by rank', 'eb49f1cc003e37b7eddf256d198e6d91c7707b2393f357bd6b6a21ce9e845f81', 8, 11, null, 32, null, null, false, null),
  ('b7302e5a-947d-5d7c-8bd0-3458be6710c4', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'development', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private-Specialist/Corporal (USAR only) — recommended publications and reading (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '(1) All Skill Level 1 CA Specialists should focus on gaining familiarization with Army and CA CMF terminology, and operations at the company and team level. Soldiers should study and master the following military publications: TC 7–21.13 (Soldier’s Guide), STP 21–1–SMCT (Soldier’s Manual of Common Task, SL1), FM 7–22 (Holistic Health and Fitness (H2F)), TC 4- 02.1 (First Aid), AR 670–1 (Wear and Appearance of Army Uniforms and Insignia), TM 9-1005- 319-10 (Operators Manual for Carbine 5.56mm, M4A1), TM 9-1005 - 470-10 (Operators Manual for MHS: M17, M18 Pistol), FM 3-25.26 (Map Reading and Land Navigation), all -10 level technical manuals associated with their equipment, and battle drills associated with their current assignment.
(2) Recommended Reading: Small Unit Leadership: A Commonsense Approach by Dandridge Malone, A Bell for Adano by John Hersey, Ender’s Game by Orson Scott Card, How to Win Friends and Influence People by Dale Carnegie.', 'dabeef07d046d397ae729b0b91136efe155308dec8455cc7ff33a5f56d1f167b', 12, 12, null, 33, null, null, false, null),
  ('fca3fa8d-4b82-554a-b1c2-b79b004dab4b', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'development', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant (USAR only) — recommended publications and reading (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '(1) Foundational: TC 7-22.7 (Noncommissioned Officer Guide), STP 21-24-SMCT (Warrior Tasks, SL 2-4), FM 3-57 (Civil Affairs Operations), ADP 1 (The Army), ADP 3-07 (Stability), AR 623-3 (Evaluation Reporting System), ADP 6-22 (Army Leadership and the Profession), AR 600-9 (The Army Body Composition Program), DA PAM 600-25 (base document and Civil Affairs Chapter), ADP 7-0 (Training) and ADP 6-0 (Mission Command).
(2) Recommended Reading: The Ugly American, Starship Troopers, Influence: The Psychology of Persuasion, Savage Wars of Peace.', '2c40a8fba57146b5a23004c27c981ddb74d179e4df956979c191f3e7b2ff2cfd', 12, 12, null, 34, null, null, false, null),
  ('d4d27494-eac2-508e-a09b-7a42ac9e5ef0', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'development', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — recommended publications and reading (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '(1) Builds Upon Sergeant: JP 3-57 (Civil-Military Operations), FM 1-02.2 (Military Symbols), FM 1-02.1 (Operational Terms), ADP 3-0 (Operations), ADP 3-05 (Army Special Operations), ADP 3-37 (Protection), ADP 7-0 (Training), FM 3-98 (Reconnaissance and Security Operations), FM 3-90-2 (Reconnaissance, Security, and Tactical Enabling Tasks Volume 2), ATP 6-22.01 (The Counseling Process), ATP 3-50.20 (SERE Planning and Preparation), ADP 5-0 (The Operations Process) for early exposure to operational planning.
(2) Recommended Reading: Talking to Strangers, Inside a U.S. Embassy: Diplomacy at Work, Leaders Eat Last, David and Goliath, The Road Not Taken, The Undoing Project, Team of Teams, This Kind of War.', '3ca98f844ed6c131a7398b20da528819a9fd3a15f612cdce15f609539a6508ad', 12, 12, null, 35, null, null, false, null),
  ('b4243e10-9ced-5c20-b333-5fa723b1c145', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — recommended publications and reading (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '(1) Builds Upon Staff Sergeant: FM 6-0 (Commander and Staff Organization and Operations) or ADP 6-0 (Mission Command), FM 3-0 (Operations), ADP 5-0 (The Operations Process), ATP 5- 19 (Risk Management), ATP 3-60 (Targeting), FM 7-22.7 (The Noncommissioned Officer Guide), TC 3-57.51 (Civil Preparation of the Battlefield), FM 7-0 (Training), FM 7-22 (Holistic Health and Fitness), FM 6-22 (Developing Leaders), FM 3-13 (Information Operations), FM 3-55 (Information Collection), ATP 2-01.3 (Intelligence Preparation of the Battlefield), AR 25-50 (Preparing and Managing Correspondence), ADP 1-01 (Doctrine Primer), ADP 2-0 (Intelligence), ADP 3-90 (Offense and Defense), ADP 4-0 (Sustainment), ATP 3-57.X series, AR 600-8-22 (Military Awards) and AR 27-10 (Military Justice).
(2) Recommended Reading: Start with Why, Outliers, The Black Swan, World Order, The Training Ground, The Third Pillar, The Generals, The History of the Peloponnesian War, The 4 Disciplines of Execution, The Prince.', 'a90cebeda2e7df3171fb47ce8ae18091fa86164b0d278421cceb69dfd6db3b1b', 12, 13, null, 36, null, null, false, null),
  ('5ef6f9d4-b08e-5cb5-b5cb-3cca6ec91f42', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant and First Sergeant — recommended publications and reading (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '(1) Builds Upon Sergeant First Class: AR 600-20 (Army Command Policy), FM 7-22 (Army Physical Readiness Training), AR 11-6 (Army Foreign Language Program), AR 15-185 (Army Board for Correction of Military Records), AR 220-1 (Army Unit Status Reporting), AR 601-280 (Army Retention Program), AR 614-200 (Enlisted Assignments and Utilization), Mastery of Army Training Network.
(2) Recommended Reading: Winning, Becoming a Resonant Leader, Mindset, Good to Great.', '399a821dc9bf14796808069fedef8c341712274cef656255af4d7eaeaa67718a', 13, 13, null, 37, null, null, false, null),
  ('a098d75f-5c17-5a6d-bfa7-2e079ae9226a', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'development', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major and Command Sergeant Major — recommended publications and reading (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '(1) Sergeants Major and Command Sergeants Major should continue to study and master regulations, policies, and doctrine as updates are published.
(2) Recommended professional reading for the CA Sergeant Major is: Once an Eagle by Anton Myrer, Thinking Fast and Slow by Daniel Kahneman, The Slight Edge: Turning Simple Disciplines into Massive Success and Happiness by Jeff Olson and John David Mann, Nudge: Improving Decisions About Health, Wealth, and Happiness by Richard M. Thaler and Cass R Sunstein.', '06cf8a73620c0a42a287bb612674d6c85280d3b701829ae4c4ea8be0867d4ceb', 13, 13, null, 38, null, null, false, null),
  ('a99484b3-951f-5cb9-9d6b-9ad3191eb004', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'development', null, null, 'Overview', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '3 - 1. Civil Affairs Soldiers are expected to succeed in missions that are often demanding and high-risk within an increasingly complex and dynamic contemporary operating environment. Therefore, the CA branch naturally produces a high concentration of highly skilled NCOs. NCOs that consistently exceed standards and demonstrate mental and physical capabilities above their peers are more competitive for demanding positions and career advancement. The Special Operations Center of Excellence, the CA Proponent, the United States Army Human Resources Command (HRC), and the NCO Professional Development System (NCOPDS) all play an important part in the career development of the force; however, each Soldier is ultimately the true steward of their career. To assist leaders with talent management, NCOs at every level must seek opportunities to demonstrate their leadership potential and distinguish themselves from their peers. Refer to DA PAM 600-25 (U.S. Army Noncommissioned Officer Professional Development Guide), para 2-15. (https://www.army.mil/g-1#org-g-1-publications) for more on Self-development. 3 - 2. Physical Fitness. The ability to conduct CAO across the competition continuum requires CA Soldiers to be physically capable of operating in the most rigorous environments. All personnel must maintain a high state of readiness. Physical fitness contributes to NCOs overall wellness and ability to set the example for others. A holistic health and fitness (H2F) approach is essential to readiness when assessing a Soldier’s physical and mental status. Additionally, it assists to design programs to sustain or improve that Soldier’s ability to meet the demands of their MOSs, duty assignments, and combat-specific tasks. While it is a command responsibility to establish physical training policies and allocate sufficient resources for Soldiers to meet Army and unit standards and goals, it is an individual responsibility to meet those goals. Soldiers must keep themselves in a high state of physical readiness. All Soldiers are encouraged to use existing resources to assist them in making a healthy lifestyle, good dietary choices, and developing an individual goal-oriented physical training regimen. CA Soldiers will meet all standards for their respective grades as specified in DA PAM 611-21. NCOs assigned to either the Active Component or Reserve Components must meet all physical fitness requirements as directed by their command to ensure maximum mission readiness to fulfill global requirements with Joint Special Operations Forces (SOF) partners and support Joint Commanders. 3 - 3. Language: Your Strategic Advantage.
(1) Fluency equals operational success: As a Civil Affairs NCO in a language-capable CMF, your language proficiency is mission-critical. Achieve and maintain the highest level of fluency possible for your assigned control language (CLANG).
(2) Cultural Expertise Amplifies Impact: Combine your language skills with a deep understanding of regional cultures, customs, values, and social norms. This enables you to connect meaningfully, build trust, and navigate complex situations.
(3) Stay Current, Stay Relevant: The world is constantly evolving. Continuously update your knowledge of regional history, current events, and emerging trends to anticipate challenges and capitalize on opportunities.
(4) Communication is Your Weapon: Leverage your language and cultural expertise to engage local populations, gather critical information, influence perceptions, and decisively achieve mission objectives.
a. Active-duty CA NCOs are required to maintain control language proficiency in accordance with AR 11-6 Army Foreign Language Program, AR 614-200 Enlisted Assignments and Utilization Management, USASOC Regulation 350-1, and applicable unit policies. Soldiers scoring under the L3/R3 DLPT level or S3 OPI level must be recertified annually. Those who score L3/R3 on the DLPT or S3 on the OPI have a biennial (every 2-years) mandatory recertification requirement. Commanders must adhere to the policies identified in this chapter to re-evaluate their assigned ARSOF Soldiers’ proficiency IAW AR 11-6 dated 25 February 2022, Chapter 5-4. Army Special Operations Forces language proficiency testing.
b. Language capable ARSOF MOSs 38Z, 38R, 38W while serving as Drill Sergeants, Recruiters, and Observer Controllers are exempt from the 12-month language currency for 36- months while serving in these positions IAW Exception to Policy Memorandum dated 07 January 2020 signed by HQDAG-2 Language Team.
c. USAR members are encouraged to seek a language through the Defense Language Institute Foreign Language Center (DLIFLC) but are not required to do so in accordance with AR 11-6. Many USAR CA positions are specifically language coded based on the unit of assignment. Training may be authorized through the DLIFLC or numerous other approved programs and venues. USAR Soldiers that have received language training are required to attend 80 hours of consecutive language training or equivalent annually. All 38B Soldiers that meet minimum proficiency standards for any foreign language are authorized Foreign Language Proficiency Pay. 3 - 4. Military Bearing. Soldiers in all ranks must maintain a high level of physical, moral, and ethical discipline. NCOs in all grades should be well-versed in Army customs, courtesies, and traditions in accordance with: AR 600-25 (Salutes, Honors, and Courtesies), AR 670-1 (Wear and Appearance of Army Uniforms and Insignia), TC 3-21.5 (Drill and Ceremonies), TC 7-21.13 (Soldier’s Guide), TC 7-22.7 (The Noncommissioned Officer Guide), and applicable unit policies. 3 - 5. Required attributes. Soldiers wishing to obtain and maintain an Active Component ARSOF CA MOS (38Z, 38R or 38W) must demonstrate and internalize the following ARSOF core attributes:
a. Integrity. Be trustworthy and honest, act with honor and unwavering adherence to ethical standards.
b. Courage. Act on own convictions despite consequences, willing to sacrifice for a larger cause, be not paralyzed by fear of failure.
c. Perseverance. Work toward an end, have commitment, physical or mental resolve, motivated, give effort to the cause, do not quit.
d. Personal Responsibility. Be self-motivated and an autonomous self-starter, anticipate tasks and act, accordingly, take accountability for his/her actions.
e. Professionalism. Be a standard-bearer for the corps, have a professional image, to include a level of maturity and judgment mixed with confidence and humility, form sound opinions and make own decisions, stand behind his/her sensible decisions based on his/her experiences.
f. Adaptability. Personnel must demonstrate a high degree of adaptability and cognitive flexibility, maintaining composure while adjusting to dynamic environments. They are expected to employ creative problem-solving and think unconventionally to overcome challenges. A critical skill is the ability to recognize, understand, and navigate complex social networks within the human domain. Finally, CA professionals must exhibit the initiative to proactively shape the operational environment and set conditions to achieve desired outcomes.
g. Team Player. Demonstrates unwavering loyalty to the team and selfless dedication to the shared mission. Dependable, driven by a strong sense of duty to their fellow team members and the task at hand. By treating everyone with dignity and respect, they foster cohesion and recognize that the diverse strengths within the team are essential to mission success and serve as a force multiplier.
h. Capability. Maintain physical fitness, to include strength and agility, maintain operational knowledge, able to plan and communicate effectively. 3 - 6. Functional Training. Civil Affairs NCOs are highly encouraged to pursue formal military training opportunities that improve their survivability, tactical, and technical skills and increase their ability to lead Soldiers in any CA unit that they may be assigned. This includes but is not limited to all Additional Skill Identifier (ASI) and Special Qualifications Identifier (SQI) Producing Schools, Joint Service Courses, and International Military/Government Training.
a. Due to the high risk of isolation within potential denied areas inherent to the operational environment in which active-duty CATs operate, units are required to provide Survival, Evasion, Resistance, and Escape (SERE-C High Risk) for every active-duty CA Soldier IAW AR 525-28 para.2-21. Additionally, over 85% of Critical Leadership Development opportunities in the active component are airborne assignments, therefore all active-duty CA NCOs should successfully complete the Static Line Jumpmaster Course at the earliest opportunity.
b. USAR CA Forces support the conventional force with a structure that includes two Airborne BDEs and subordinate BNs and other Airborne coded positions. Individuals in these assignments must attend the Basic Airborne Course and maintain current jump status to be fully qualified for the position. Prior to assignment in a paid parachute position, non-airborne qualified TPU and AGR Soldiers should complete an Airborne Training Volunteer Statement. All NCOs in an airborne assignment should attend the Static Line Jump Master Course at the earliest opportunity. USAR Soldiers should also pursue job specific and functional courses that enhance professional technical skills and knowledge at various schools including the Army Reserve Readiness Training Center (ARRTC). Unless directed, USAR Soldiers do not have a SERE-C requirement. 3 - 7. Additional Military Educational Opportunities. Soldiers of all ranks are highly encouraged to continue their military training and education through self-paced and distributed-learning platforms. Courses in a wide variety of military topics can be found at the Army Training Information System (ATIS) Learning, Joint Knowledge Online (JKO), and Joint Special Operations University.', '9f27e9104f4abfa3cacf1a35e0d91ea660c165b1db6da47d986e994dd44426e8', 5, 7, null, 39, null, null, false, null),
  ('53884fa3-23d2-548b-95e8-205a268093b2', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'development', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private-Specialist/Corporal — recommended professional military education (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', 'Decentralized promotion eligibility requirements for USAR Soldiers in the ranks of PVT, PV2, PFC, and SPC are outlined in AR 600-8-19, chapter 2.', '09c82084e48da7cd471be051b8dad473bc2e50406514bd85983510ff69ef7b46', 8, 8, null, 40, null, null, false, null),
  ('f83bad90-615c-56e5-9c53-fbca0bc8893a', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'development', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — recommended professional military education (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', 'Consideration by a local promotion board is mandatory for all Soldiers upon initially reaching primary zone eligibility.', 'e91be1813c1d62927b40eb321fe5f9af2912b38c4611e2ec983b85b0ec4eb022', 8, 8, null, 41, null, null, false, null),
  ('29832a33-e938-54fb-95bd-15f24489daad', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'development', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — recommended professional military education (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', 'Active Component and AGR Staff Sergeants serving in a SOF unit should strive to complete the Joint Special Operations University Joint Fundamentals Course (CEP 1) and the JKO Enlisted Joint Professional Military Education Course (EJPME I). Staff Sergeants must take the initiative to become familiar with the Army Training Network and the Army Training Information System.', '8d3bfae76552c678c7759d9799d7802126efc414ab1fd29870f4d7333140bdab', 8, 8, null, 42, null, null, false, null),
  ('1245af83-1080-57e7-a718-e76c5b828315', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — recommended professional military education (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', 'Active Component and AGR Sergeants First Class serving in a SOF unit should strive to complete the Joint Special Operations University Enterprise Management Course (CEP 2) and the JKO Enlisted Joint Professional Military Education Course (EJPME II).', '48400fe1cf2e22ec7b333e457ea9ce1d8d6f5550c399124e5c151d8384d1ce84', 8, 8, null, 43, null, null, false, null),
  ('9de4ccca-0c0d-553e-9c49-e1e258ac8d92', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant — recommended professional military education (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', 'Master Sergeants and First Sergeants should strive to complete the JKO Enlisted Joint Professional Military Education Course (EJPME II) if not already complete.', 'e35ac6c23beb48d51ba6f0074a509453cb0dc9bfbfd8cf634cd1d70a107f780c', 8, 8, null, 44, null, null, false, null),
  ('ac841381-aada-5b40-9ca3-2cc0e3876541', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'development', null, null, 'General guidance (broadening billets, competitive boards, education resources, professional reading)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '3 - 8. Broadening billets and assignments. This covers a wide range of positions and duties outside of one’s MOS. CA Enlisted and NCO assignments are classified into three basic categories: Critical Leadership Developmental (CD), Developmental, and Broadening. All assignments are developmental.
a. Critical Leadership assignments are vital for professional development, providing a foundational understanding of CA operations while serving as the framework to assess a Soldier''s potential for career advancement.
b. Developmental assignments are those assignments that enhance warfighting capability but are not deemed so fundamental to CA Soldiers or NCOs progression that they qualify as a Critical Leadership assignment.
c. Broadening assignments are those assignments that provide a purposeful expansion of knowledge and capability and are fundamental to the continued growth throughout the CA Soldiers or NCOs career lifecycle. For any position to be considered broadening, it must either be an operational or institutional position in an organization at the operational or strategic levels providing an experience above and beyond the tactical level or an MOS-immaterial position that increases skill in Army policy and programs outside of their CMF. Assignments and billets include, but are not limited to, assignments at a Theater Special Operations Command TSOC, GCC, USASOC, 1st SFC (A), Multi-Domain Effects Battalion (MDEB), Multi-Domain Task Force (MDTF), Theater Information Advantage Detachment (TIAD), Division or CORP G-9, SFG S-9, OSC or positions such as Observer Controller/Trainer (JRTC/NTC), Drill Sergeant, Recruiter (including SORB), Inspector General (IG) NCO, Brigade level Sexual Assault Response Coordinator or Sexual Harassment/Assault Response and Prevention Coordinator, and EOA assignments. Broadening often occurs following Critical Leadership assignments. The full list of assignments can be found in the paragraphs below for each grade plate. To be considered ''Best Qualified'' and competitive for future promotions, CA Soldiers must complete their Critical Leadership assignment, and all required professional military education at each grade. 3 - 9. Competitive Boards. Soldiers at all ranks are encouraged to surpass standards in presence, intellect, leadership, development, and achievement, setting the example for their peers and subordinates through courage, confidence, competence, military bearing, general military knowledge, and warrior skills. Those seeking to challenge themselves and excel beyond their peers should consider competing in events such as: Active Component Best Civil Affairs Competition, U.S. Army Best Medic Competition, U.S. Army Best Ranger Competition, U.S. Army Best Sapper Competition, U.S. Army Best Warrior/Best Squad Competition, Soldier and NCO of the Month, Quarter, and Year boards. NCOs at all ranks are encouraged to seek acceptance into the prestigious Sergeant Audie Murphy or Sergeant Morales Clubs. 3 - 10. Credentials/Certifications. Must maintain a current, valid, unrestricted National Registry Emergency Medical Technician Certification and Advanced Tactical Paramedic Certification to retain MOS 38W or W4 ASI. 3 - 11. Civilian Education. Civilian education is a vital component of self-development for Civil Affairs Soldiers. Prioritizing education early in your career accelerates professional growth and enhances flexibility in navigating operational demands. Although education is an important factor enhancing potential value to the Army, comparison of SMs based on civilian education attainments should be viewed as an indicator of individual initiative rather than as a prerequisite for selection as most/highly qualified. College is favorable to set SMs apart from their peers, but it is not a requirement for promotion. Soldiers should pursue degree programs relevant to their MOS to maximize credit for prior military training. Explore the Service-member Opportunity Colleges Army Degree (SOCAD) website for more information, https://www.education.army.mil/socad.aspx. For details on obtaining college credits, refer to paragraph 10.
a. Staff Sergeants should aim to complete an associate degree, or at a minimum complete 60 semester hours. Staff Sergeants that have already completed a bachelor’s degree are highly competitive to pursue a master’s degree with. Civil Affairs Medical Sergeants (38W) students are enrolled into the Uniformed Services University (USU) College of Allied Health Sciences (CAHS) upon admittance to the Special Operation Medical Sergeants Course. Upon graduation of the Special Operations Combat Medical Course, a medic is awarded the Associates of Science (AS) in Health Science from the Uniformed Services University (USU). The primary location of training within USASOC is the Joint Special Operations Medical Training Center.
b. Sergeants First Class should have already completed an associate degree or 90 semester hours by 13 years TIS and should have had the opportunity to work towards or complete their bachelor’s degree. Sergeants First Class who have already completed a bachelor’s degree are highly encouraged to pursue a master’s degree with National Defense University (NDU).
c. Proponent note. National Defense University (NDU). Selected NCOs attend the highly competitive Joint Special Operations Master of Arts program conducted over ten months at Fort Bragg, NC, producing a Master of Arts in Strategic Security Studies. This education better prepares CA NCOs for assignments in Army Educational Requirements System (AERS) coded “98” billets. The gaining command has the responsibility to ensure NCOs assigned against validated AERS positions are properly utilized. Per the regulation (AR 621-1 para 4-5a). Graduates of the program have enhanced strategic situational awareness and are better prepared for the complexities of special operations in interagency and multinational contexts. The application for NDU requires endorsement by the first O-6 in the officer’s chain of command. Graduates of this venue incur a one to two ratio utilization duration tour per AR 621-1 and AR 350-100.
d. Proponent note. NCOs who want to attend must have completed appropriate Noncommissioned Officer educational programs for grade and years of service. NCOs normally must have no less than 6 years-time in service or no more than 13 years-time in service (TIS) as of the report date to the academic institution. NCOs with more than 13-year TIS (waiver possible) will be considered on a case-by-case basis. Must be indefinite or have sufficient enlistment remaining equal to or greater than three times the length of requested schooling. Must be in the grade of E7 or above.
e. Master Sergeants and First Sergeants should have at least 120 semester hours of civilian college education towards a bachelor or higher-level degree. There are certain positions or billets that may require a master’s degree to apply. Master Sergeants and First Sergeants should complete a bachelor’s degree and be willing to work towards a master’s degree while serving in the assigned billet.
f. Sergeants Major and Command Sergeants Major should have a bachelor’s degree and are highly encouraged to obtain a master’s degree. Joint Special Operations University courses, especially CEP 4 (Enlisted Academy Summit course) will become available if selected to serve at the nominative level. Due to limited authorizations and fiercely competitive records, civilian education may be considered a major factor for selection to Command Sergeant Major (CSM). There are certain positions or billets that may require a master’s degree to apply. At a minimum, Sergeants Major must have completed a bachelor’s degree and be willing to work towards a master’s degree while serving in a AERS 98 coded billet. 3 - 12. College Opportunities. Many opportunities and resources exist to support CA Soldiers in pursuing their civilian education: Army Continuing Education System, Army University, Defense Activity for Non-Traditional Education Support, Army IgnitED, Joint Service Transcript, and Advanced Civil Schooling opportunities. Achievement of college or higher-level degree credits can also be completed during the MOS training process, as modeled by the 38W SOCM program degree.
a. Army Continuing Education System (ACES). ACES programs and services support the self- development domain of the Army leader development tenets by providing Soldiers opportunities for traditional and technology-based education and support for personal and professional career goals. The ACES programs assist Soldiers in achieving the lifelong synthesis of the knowledge, skills, and experiences required for success. Enlisted Soldiers should master the academic skills needed to perform the duties of their primary military occupational specialty and to meet prerequisites for the Noncommissioned Officer Professional Development System (NCOPDS) in accordance with AR 621-5.
b. The DOD MWR Libraries virtual collection includes e-books, audiobooks, academic support, test prep, professional development, and a vast array of learning resources and online content. This platform provides free access to resources including but not limited to, Mango Languages, Universal Calss, EBSCO Learning Express, Gale OneFile: Military and Intelligence, and World Book Student. You can access these materials by going to https://army.dodmwrlibraries.org and logging in with your DoWID number and birth date.
c. Army University. The Army University is a professional military education university system of the United States Army. It is the most extensive professional military education system globally with over 150,000 soldiers educated in more than 88 occupations across its worldwide network of 70 schools. https://armyuniversity.edu/.
d. Defense Activity for Non-Traditional Education Support. Soldiers are encouraged to earn additional college credit for knowledge acquired outside of the traditional classroom through the Defense Activity for Non-Traditional Education Support (DANTES) https://www.dantes.mil/. College Level Examination Program (CLEP) and DANTES Subject Standardized Tests (DSST) are exams that test the mastery of college-level subjects acquired a variety of ways–through general academic instructions, significant independent study, or extracurricular work.
e. Army IgnitED. Army IgnitED is a virtual gateway for all eligible Soldiers to request college tuition assistance or funding for professional credentials and certificates online for classroom and distance learning. It allows Soldiers to manage their education records including college classes, testing, and Army Education Counselor support. Through Online programs, Education Centers and On-site programs offer over 300,000+ college courses. Credentialing Opportunity Online Learning (COOL). COOL helps Army service members find information on certifications and licenses related to their military occupation and civilian career. This assists in the operational aspect when working with unified action partners. There are 35-line areas of certifications for the CA NCO that are applicable to their skills. https://www.cool.osd.mil/army/index.html.
f. Joint Service Transcript. Joint Service Transcript is an academically accepted document approved by the American Council on Education (ACE) to validate a service member''s military occupational experience and training along with the corresponding ACE college credit recommendations. Joint Service Transcript can be accessed at https://jst.doded.mil/jst/home.
g. Advanced Civil Schooling. Advanced Civil Schooling opportunities are available for active component CA NCOs. The National Defense University (NDU) Joint Special Operations Master of Arts Strategic Security Studies degree program is conducted under the auspices of the U.S. Army John F. Kennedy Special Warfare Center and School (USAJFKSWCS) provides a Master of Science. 38Zs with a minimum of six years’ Time in Service (TIS) and no more than 13 years TIS are eligible. Application requirements are published annually.
h. Proponent note. NCOs who received an advanced degree through an Army civilian schooling program are required to serve in an initial AERS validated position for a minimum of 24 months per AR 621-1 MAY 2024. 3 - 13. Professional Reading. Civil Affairs Soldiers of all ranks should pursue self-development and understanding of the Army profession through professional reading. The Sergeant Major of the Army reading list, published by the Association of the United States Army NCO reading list broken down by rank: https://www.ausa.org/nco-reading-list and https://www.ausa.org/nco- soldier-programs. 3 - 14. Guided Self-development by rank', 'eb49f1cc003e37b7eddf256d198e6d91c7707b2393f357bd6b6a21ce9e845f81', 8, 11, null, 45, null, null, false, null),
  ('0bce9947-0a3a-553a-a86f-7f43e02a2d16', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'development', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Private-Specialist/Corporal (USAR only) — recommended publications and reading (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '(1) All Skill Level 1 CA Specialists should focus on gaining familiarization with Army and CA CMF terminology, and operations at the company and team level. Soldiers should study and master the following military publications: TC 7–21.13 (Soldier’s Guide), STP 21–1–SMCT (Soldier’s Manual of Common Task, SL1), FM 7–22 (Holistic Health and Fitness (H2F)), TC 4- 02.1 (First Aid), AR 670–1 (Wear and Appearance of Army Uniforms and Insignia), TM 9-1005- 319-10 (Operators Manual for Carbine 5.56mm, M4A1), TM 9-1005 - 470-10 (Operators Manual for MHS: M17, M18 Pistol), FM 3-25.26 (Map Reading and Land Navigation), all -10 level technical manuals associated with their equipment, and battle drills associated with their current assignment.
(2) Recommended Reading: Small Unit Leadership: A Commonsense Approach by Dandridge Malone, A Bell for Adano by John Hersey, Ender’s Game by Orson Scott Card, How to Win Friends and Influence People by Dale Carnegie.', 'dabeef07d046d397ae729b0b91136efe155308dec8455cc7ff33a5f56d1f167b', 12, 12, null, 46, null, null, false, null),
  ('b80bc6a6-8c11-571a-b090-651e2ac27074', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'development', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant (USAR only) — recommended publications and reading (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '(1) Foundational: TC 7-22.7 (Noncommissioned Officer Guide), STP 21-24-SMCT (Warrior Tasks, SL 2-4), FM 3-57 (Civil Affairs Operations), ADP 1 (The Army), ADP 3-07 (Stability), AR 623-3 (Evaluation Reporting System), ADP 6-22 (Army Leadership and the Profession), AR 600-9 (The Army Body Composition Program), DA PAM 600-25 (base document and Civil Affairs Chapter), ADP 7-0 (Training) and ADP 6-0 (Mission Command).
(2) Recommended Reading: The Ugly American, Starship Troopers, Influence: The Psychology of Persuasion, Savage Wars of Peace.', '2c40a8fba57146b5a23004c27c981ddb74d179e4df956979c191f3e7b2ff2cfd', 12, 12, null, 47, null, null, false, null),
  ('4b957cda-1f45-5d21-a071-3179bb622944', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'development', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — recommended publications and reading (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '(1) Builds Upon Sergeant: JP 3-57 (Civil-Military Operations), FM 1-02.2 (Military Symbols), FM 1-02.1 (Operational Terms), ADP 3-0 (Operations), ADP 3-05 (Army Special Operations), ADP 3-37 (Protection), ADP 7-0 (Training), FM 3-98 (Reconnaissance and Security Operations), FM 3-90-2 (Reconnaissance, Security, and Tactical Enabling Tasks Volume 2), ATP 6-22.01 (The Counseling Process), ATP 3-50.20 (SERE Planning and Preparation), ADP 5-0 (The Operations Process) for early exposure to operational planning.
(2) Recommended Reading: Talking to Strangers, Inside a U.S. Embassy: Diplomacy at Work, Leaders Eat Last, David and Goliath, The Road Not Taken, The Undoing Project, Team of Teams, This Kind of War.', '3ca98f844ed6c131a7398b20da528819a9fd3a15f612cdce15f609539a6508ad', 12, 12, null, 48, null, null, false, null),
  ('2d37e380-d8ab-559e-b34f-fb175b0a4490', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — recommended publications and reading (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '(1) Builds Upon Staff Sergeant: FM 6-0 (Commander and Staff Organization and Operations) or ADP 6-0 (Mission Command), FM 3-0 (Operations), ADP 5-0 (The Operations Process), ATP 5- 19 (Risk Management), ATP 3-60 (Targeting), FM 7-22.7 (The Noncommissioned Officer Guide), TC 3-57.51 (Civil Preparation of the Battlefield), FM 7-0 (Training), FM 7-22 (Holistic Health and Fitness), FM 6-22 (Developing Leaders), FM 3-13 (Information Operations), FM 3-55 (Information Collection), ATP 2-01.3 (Intelligence Preparation of the Battlefield), AR 25-50 (Preparing and Managing Correspondence), ADP 1-01 (Doctrine Primer), ADP 2-0 (Intelligence), ADP 3-90 (Offense and Defense), ADP 4-0 (Sustainment), ATP 3-57.X series, AR 600-8-22 (Military Awards) and AR 27-10 (Military Justice).
(2) Recommended Reading: Start with Why, Outliers, The Black Swan, World Order, The Training Ground, The Third Pillar, The Generals, The History of the Peloponnesian War, The 4 Disciplines of Execution, The Prince.', 'a90cebeda2e7df3171fb47ce8ae18091fa86164b0d278421cceb69dfd6db3b1b', 12, 13, null, 49, null, null, false, null),
  ('808d108c-59e3-5ed3-ad07-db876858e62a', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant and First Sergeant — recommended publications and reading (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '(1) Builds Upon Sergeant First Class: AR 600-20 (Army Command Policy), FM 7-22 (Army Physical Readiness Training), AR 11-6 (Army Foreign Language Program), AR 15-185 (Army Board for Correction of Military Records), AR 220-1 (Army Unit Status Reporting), AR 601-280 (Army Retention Program), AR 614-200 (Enlisted Assignments and Utilization), Mastery of Army Training Network.
(2) Recommended Reading: Winning, Becoming a Resonant Leader, Mindset, Good to Great.', '399a821dc9bf14796808069fedef8c341712274cef656255af4d7eaeaa67718a', 13, 13, null, 50, null, null, false, null),
  ('b9e4ae4e-7b6d-5b03-9947-87de02acbf82', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'development', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major and Command Sergeant Major — recommended publications and reading (applies to every MOS in this career management field)', 'Chapter 3. Recommended Career Management Self-Development, by Rank', '(1) Sergeants Major and Command Sergeants Major should continue to study and master regulations, policies, and doctrine as updates are published.
(2) Recommended professional reading for the CA Sergeant Major is: Once an Eagle by Anton Myrer, Thinking Fast and Slow by Daniel Kahneman, The Slight Edge: Turning Simple Disciplines into Massive Success and Happiness by Jeff Olson and John David Mann, Nudge: Improving Decisions About Health, Wealth, and Happiness by Richard M. Thaler and Cass R Sunstein.', '06cf8a73620c0a42a287bb612674d6c85280d3b701829ae4c4ea8be0867d4ceb', 13, 13, null, 51, null, null, false, null),
  ('36154f8c-358d-52ce-bcc3-1fecc9123eca', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'development', null, null, 'Overview', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', '4 - 1. Proponent note. Proponent guidance should not be construed as a simplified checklist for the evaluation process, nor does it guarantee selection for PME. No school or assignment is a deciding factor in the merit-based evaluation-based system. Those NCOs that have completed assignments in their Critical Leadership Developmental positions and consistently outperformed their peers, receiving exceptional rater and senior rater evaluations will be viewed favorably by board members.', '32e70db787367e568cfcc07f17d2c9ae7fb77c0eff424870aee219e90a684052', 13, 13, null, 52, null, null, false, null),
  ('9b11a148-f546-51a1-801a-328f6a366d68', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'development', null, null, 'Goals for development', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'Civil Affairs CMF guidance is for Noncommissioned Officers to serve in Critical Leadership Developmental assignments at each grade to be considered CMF developed. Leaders in conjunction with ACT and DA PAM 600-25 should provide sound, reference-based counsel to CA NCOs of all ranks on their professional growth and development. All CA enlisted personnel should be enrolled in and actively utilizing ACT as a tool to monitor and guide career progression both for themselves and their subordinates.', 'cc39524ee7455fa3b7f443511344f2f7da25a12255fe52c2c3ba7bdfc7bb42a6', 14, 14, null, 53, null, null, false, null),
  ('68747327-88d4-58f6-98af-2ec965030c99', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'development', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant (SGT) — Additional guidance', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'There are no authorized 38R or 38W Sergeant positions in the Active Component. All Soldiers who graduate from CA Training Pathway and meet the requirements outlined in AR 600-8-19: Chapter 3-1 will be promoted to the rank of Staff Sergeant.', 'e046960a579ff9bcabf14f6c7d6eb550d6faf026a17f527ee8acbf237d0c1d39', 14, 14, null, 54, null, null, false, null),
  ('156a9d45-6cd6-53e2-9418-f1f35631c62f', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'development', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant (SGT) — Proponent note', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'In accordance with the exception to policy from USAJFKSWCS to the Office of the Deputy Chief of Staff, G1 to current promotion policy AR 600-8-19 authorizes automatic promotion to Staff Sergeant without regard to TIG or TIS.', 'e7fba0198b840e7bbf81e37226d50f1fdc6e6f7d08047df64a49ada7bb053492', 14, 14, null, 55, null, null, false, null),
  ('1feaa2e1-4ea3-56a7-815e-28323aec985e', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'training', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant (SGT) — Professional Military Education', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'Advanced Leader Course (ALC), ALC requirements are currently fulfilled in the Civil Affairs Training Pathway.', 'a6f5c82d861054b6b7b0c6df20d9bab3baf7d90e0fb0f1772d42eb16b3f69807', 15, 15, null, 56, null, null, false, null),
  ('ab75f4b1-e337-5bc5-a154-404a5cd5d8ef', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'MOS 38R3O Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (SSG) — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'A Staff Sergeant''s Critical Leadership Developmental position is Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant on a Civil Affairs Team (CAT). Without a minimum of 36-months of successful service on a CAT, opportunities for broadening assignments or promotion to Sergeant First Class will be limited.', 'e2c735eb90a0390ec7badb07e504455b4cc5bb51d72614cde78c43ec3f4d368d', 15, 15, null, 57, null, null, false, null),
  ('af3a58af-119b-5063-96d9-2cfaf282b8d2', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'MOS 38R3O Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (SSG) — Developmental Assignments', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'Assistant Operations NCO/Readiness NCO/Collective training NCO at the brigade/battalion/company level, Advanced Combative Instructor, BN Air Operations NCO, Civil Network Analyst NCO.', '1663e0bb414ed79aefd6a095c580d9ec1e9d6a2393cdc8515d9a6b84179005e1', 15, 15, null, 58, null, null, false, null),
  ('d0550fc4-6fc1-534f-b17e-ff59a905bfc1', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'MOS 38R3O Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (SSG) — Generating Force Assignments', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'Instructor/Writer (Civil Affairs Assessment and Selection Assessor/SERE Instructor/ARSOF BLC Instructor/Brighton Instructor/Touchstone Instructor).', '2e231ebeb0ae86be8527e9e54433bb41b8269d42a717d6ffc3173dfa16c16695', 15, 15, null, 59, null, null, false, null),
  ('cb345418-898c-56a3-81e5-4f85ffa8d939', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'development', 'E-6', null, 'MOS 38R3O Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (SSG) — Proponent note', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'Staff Sergeants must serve a minimum of 36 months in the Operational Force at their current grade to gain experience before being assigned to the Generating Force.', '62a31b8b5efedae6bc6412260fbf40cf10beab1aaa4804012d1a8f733f51a529', 15, 15, null, 60, null, null, false, null),
  ('0c9b3f49-6e04-56a5-a90b-e36709c2fa68', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'MOS 38R3O Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (SSG) — Broadening billets and assignments', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'These include, but are not limited to, assignment at a TIAD, TSOC, USASOC,1st SFC (A), Office of Security and Cooperation (OSC), or positions such as Observer Controller/Trainer (JRTC/NTC), and Special Operations Recruiting Battalion (SORB) Recruiter.', '5e20cf5ef5836856a61456a87b56e4a5ca12eeb8ed5824bda5d0084df49ace54', 15, 15, null, 61, null, null, false, null),
  ('18aa65c0-5886-5528-85b1-3c63831a1425', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'MOS 38R3O Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (SSG) — Nominative billets', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'Fewer than 5% of Staff Sergeants are selected for highly competitive nominative positions, such as Recruiter, which requires a rigorous selection process. These NCOs are chosen for their superior performance records and exemplary legal, moral, and ethical standards. It is essential that those who continue to excel in these demanding roles are given strong consideration for promotion, as their achievements directly reflect the highest standards of CMF 38.', '73225626d5165c73214ec207309ac6d8333e30512f0fdcd4fa70bb60659610ca', 15, 15, null, 62, null, null, false, null),
  ('4fd7e56b-ef9b-54fb-95e8-0bc810453657', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'training', 'E-6', null, 'MOS 38R3O Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (SSG) — Professional Military Education', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'Senior Leader Course: Obtaining recognition as the Distinguished Honor Graduate, Honor Graduate or Commandant’s List at Professional Military Education (PME) courses, or any MOS enhancing courses attended should be the goal of all CA NCOs.', 'd696b7eb5c7f23eec975ed3f526f89143d53c205348581f404c76e04cfb76f7b', 15, 15, null, 63, null, null, false, null),
  ('e6f7d7a6-5aee-5515-84f6-14610d8ae532', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'development', 'E-6', null, 'MOS 38R3O Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (SSG) — Institutional Domain Training', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'SERE-C (High Risk), Static Line Jumpmaster Course, Ranger, Sapper Leader Course, Reconnaissance and Surveillance Leaders Course (RSLC), Special Warfare Touchstone, Special Warfare Brighton, Critical Infrastructure Course, Battle Staff NCO Course, Special Warfare Network Development Course (SWNDC), Robotics and Unmanned Systems Integration Course (RUSIC).', 'f0fcbd5144ea8f99f0ac41a9e74d26851dbc04bac3dfdecf05cbd14b1d698474', 15, 15, null, 64, null, null, false, null),
  ('4e75a355-4573-5910-bfc9-41514b64ed03', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'development', 'E-6', null, 'MOS 38R3O Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (SSG) — Proponent note', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'The Critical Infrastructure Course is a non-ATRRS accredited course, thus a letter must be sent to the evaluation board with a certificate of completion to receive credit during the board process.', '353ac2faecddba2a121e3fc0a5a5170672c68d15ed85f71a26c800054a75826b', 15, 15, null, 65, null, null, false, null),
  ('57711b2c-e676-57ab-b353-c2e14b1e1c9a', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'development', 'E-6', null, 'MOS 38R3O Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (SSG) — Operational Domain', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'Competitive boards/competitions, Expert Infantryman/Soldier/Field Medical Badge (E3B).', 'c88b0d7e5337dd8b52c41eecf534cc86587d1465a4d5f0af9f6f9bf9ef8b020d', 16, 16, null, 66, null, null, false, null),
  ('33af4057-57aa-5c56-9d84-9b3c99aacf77', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38R', null, 'promotion_criteria', 'E-6', null, 'MOS 38R3O Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (SSG) — promotion consideration', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'm. Proponent guidance:
(1) Most Qualified (MQ).
(a) Displayed superior performance while serving a minimum of 36-months rated time on a Civil Affairs Team.
(b) Physical Fitness. Must meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and pass the AFT with a total score of 450 or better with a minimum of 90 points in each event in accordance with Army Directive 2025-06.
(c) Met language proficiency standards (per AR 11-6, 1-15 and USASOC Reg 350-1) by scoring 1+ on Oral Proficiency Interview (16 in Speak) or 1+/1+ on the Defense Language Proficiency Test (16 in Read and Listen) within 12-months as reflected on the Soldier Talent Profile.
(d) Must be SERE-C (High Risk) qualified.
(e) Complete one of the following courses: Static Line Jumpmaster Course, Ranger School, Sapper Leader Course, Reconnaissance and Surveillance Leaders Course.
(f) A pattern of clearly stated strong, quantifiable ratings and enumerations by the Senior Rater that denotes promote ahead of peers and displays the superior potential to serve at the next higher grade.
(2) Fully Qualified (FQ).
(a) Displayed satisfactory performance while serving a minimum of 24-months rated time on a Civil Affairs Team.
(b) Physical Fitness. Met Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and pass the AFT with a total score of 400 or better with a minimum of 80 points in each event in accordance with Army Directive 2025-06 or be on a current profile.
(c) Met language proficiency standards per AR 11-6, 1-15 and USASOC Reg 350-1) by scoring 1+ on Oral Proficiency Interview (16 in Speak) or 1+/1+ on the Defense Language Proficiency Test (16 in Read and Listen) within 12-months as reflected on the Soldier Talent Profile.
(d) Must be SERE-C (High Risk) qualified.
(e) A pattern of clearly stated satisfactory, quantifiable ratings and enumerations by the Senior Rater that denotes promote with peers and displays potential to serve at the next higher grade.
(3) Not Fully Qualified (NFQ).
(a) Has not completed a minimum of 24-months rated time on a Civil Affairs Team.
(b) Physical Fitness. Does not meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 or has not passed the AFT with a total score of 400 or better with a minimum of 80 points in each event in accordance with Army Directive 2025-06 or be on a current profile.
(c) Scored a 1 or less on the Oral Proficiency Interview (Speak) or Defense Language Proficiency Test (Read and Listen) within 12-months as reflected on the Soldier Talent Profile.
(d) Not SERE-C (High Risk) qualified.
(4) Not Fully Qualified for Retention (NFQ-R).
(a). The following must have occurred within the current grade and the 18 months prior to the most current evaluation board:
(1) Leadership: Received a GOMOR or conviction by court-martial in current grade or received a Bar to continued service IAW AR 601-28.
(2) Received NCOER in current grade with any of the following:
(a) Relief for Cause.
(b) Annotation of “Did Not Meet Standard” in Part IV (Performance Evaluation, Professionalism, Attributes, and Competencies), block c (Character).
(c) Annotation of “Not Qualified” in Part V (Senior Rater Overall Potential), block a (Rated Noncommissioned Officer’s Potential).
(3) Assignments: Declines to serve on a CA unit of action, directed assignment, or is unable to meet assignment requirements due to loss of security clearance, flags, or adverse actions.
(4) Military Education: Refuses to complete PME, and/or consistently fails to meet the prerequisites to attend PME or advance skill courses.
(5) Physical Fitness: Fails to score 300 points with a minimum of 60 points in each event on the AFT in accordance with Army Directive 2025-06, effective 1 June 2025, and/or fails to meet height / weight requirements IAW AR 600-9.
(b) Proponent note. Language capable ARSOF MOSs 38Z, 38R, 38W while serving as Drill Sergeants, Recruiters, and Observer Controllers are exempt from the 12-month language currency for 36 months while serving in these positions IAW Exception to Policy Memorandum dated 07 January 2020 signed by HQDA G-2 Language Team.
(c) Proponent note. Those who score L3/R3 on the DLPT or S3 on the OPI have a biennial (every 2-years) mandatory recertification requirement. Commanders must adhere to the policies identified in this chapter to re-evaluate their assigned ARSOF Soldiers’ proficiency IAW AR 11-6 dated 25 February 2022, Chapter 5-4. Army Special Operations Forces language proficiency testing.', 'a3192f53fb4707abb929b2ea30503793e1635928e46d1212f6b87075a00e1ea8', 16, 17, null, 67, null, null, false, null),
  ('1870fe8e-7dc7-5ca1-b203-74ffd6a9ca12', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'development', null, null, 'Overview', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', '4 - 1. Proponent note. Proponent guidance should not be construed as a simplified checklist for the evaluation process, nor does it guarantee selection for PME. No school or assignment is a deciding factor in the merit-based evaluation-based system. Those NCOs that have completed assignments in their Critical Leadership Developmental positions and consistently outperformed their peers, receiving exceptional rater and senior rater evaluations will be viewed favorably by board members.', '32e70db787367e568cfcc07f17d2c9ae7fb77c0eff424870aee219e90a684052', 13, 13, null, 68, null, null, false, null),
  ('bb08b578-bf3b-59be-84b2-7d8c685fa197', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'development', null, null, 'Goals for development', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'Civil Affairs CMF guidance is for Noncommissioned Officers to serve in Critical Leadership Developmental assignments at each grade to be considered CMF developed. Leaders in conjunction with ACT and DA PAM 600-25 should provide sound, reference-based counsel to CA NCOs of all ranks on their professional growth and development. All CA enlisted personnel should be enrolled in and actively utilizing ACT as a tool to monitor and guide career progression both for themselves and their subordinates.', 'cc39524ee7455fa3b7f443511344f2f7da25a12255fe52c2c3ba7bdfc7bb42a6', 14, 14, null, 69, null, null, false, null),
  ('d2b4e218-9f4d-5286-af2e-4b0e67ead3ba', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'development', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant (SGT) — Additional guidance', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'There are no authorized 38R or 38W Sergeant positions in the Active Component. All Soldiers who graduate from CA Training Pathway and meet the requirements outlined in AR 600-8-19: Chapter 3-1 will be promoted to the rank of Staff Sergeant.', 'e046960a579ff9bcabf14f6c7d6eb550d6faf026a17f527ee8acbf237d0c1d39', 14, 14, null, 70, null, null, false, null),
  ('37840c44-c8dc-563a-a61c-461d90738ed0', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'development', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant (SGT) — Proponent note', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'In accordance with the exception to policy from USAJFKSWCS to the Office of the Deputy Chief of Staff, G1 to current promotion policy AR 600-8-19 authorizes automatic promotion to Staff Sergeant without regard to TIG or TIS.', 'e7fba0198b840e7bbf81e37226d50f1fdc6e6f7d08047df64a49ada7bb053492', 14, 14, null, 71, null, null, false, null),
  ('9ee725d6-fa7b-54a6-9856-c02bf38278f9', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'training', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant (SGT) — Professional Military Education', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'Advanced Leader Course (ALC), ALC requirements are currently fulfilled in the Civil Affairs Training Pathway.', 'a6f5c82d861054b6b7b0c6df20d9bab3baf7d90e0fb0f1772d42eb16b3f69807', 15, 15, null, 72, null, null, false, null),
  ('190dd80a-9c5a-5036-bdee-3a94a9da4ffd', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'MOS 38W3O Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (SSG) — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'A Staff Sergeant''s Critical Leadership Developmental position is Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant on a Civil Affairs Team. Without a minimum of 36-months of successful service on a CAT, opportunities for broadening assignments or promotion to Sergeant First Class will be limited.', 'b20e333ed69d21efa9f038f179cfbb6cb7804c9e8d0bbc3583e8c4a6dc470413', 18, 18, null, 73, null, null, false, null),
  ('2c11eca7-0dff-5142-92de-64dfcfe719d0', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'MOS 38W3O Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (SSG) — Developmental Assignments', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant/Training Development NCO/Taskings NCO/Readiness NCO at the brigade/battalion/company level.', 'ccf0d3d63255de7f320cafaaf1d80d08cb22022291357298b1ce62aa6374f2a4', 18, 18, null, 74, null, null, false, null),
  ('86d0eee3-7abc-5baf-ad62-76ac5bf110aa', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'MOS 38W3O Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (SSG) — Generating Force Assignments', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'Instructor/Writer (Civil Affairs Assessment and Selection Assessor/38W Phase1 Instructor/SERE Instructor/ARSOF BLC Instructor/Brighton Instructor/Touchstone Instructor).', 'd30bff79352baf8c31d9cde847a32b3d4e6a646bb0972203efa86d7599648cda', 18, 18, null, 75, null, null, false, null),
  ('fad23217-17d0-5a05-ab4a-eac63a6f8674', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'development', 'E-6', null, 'MOS 38W3O Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (SSG) — Proponent note', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'Staff Sergeants must serve a minimum of 36 months in the Operational Force at their current grade to gain experience before being assigned to the Generating/Institutional/Training Force.', '9b1b62351078681f4e1d06a22966794ebeae8599afa4b606892b7127ab43130b', 18, 18, null, 76, null, null, false, null),
  ('5cc6f851-7f4c-5da1-b693-6f891c5b3f0f', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'MOS 38W3O Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (SSG) — Broadening billets and assignments', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'These include, but are not limited to, assignments at a Theater Special Operations Command, USASOC, 1st SFC (A), OSC, or positions such as Observer Controller/Trainer (JRTC/NTC), SORB Recruiter.', '309a999bde18d04059ee58fc0920bf5801842da87260f4f76736c6890c619340', 18, 18, null, 77, null, null, false, null),
  ('89e62170-807c-50b6-afab-0da788d3ec2d', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'MOS 38W3O Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (SSG) — Nominative billets', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'Fewer than 5% of Staff Sergeants are selected for highly competitive nominative positions, such as Recruiter, which requires a rigorous selection process. These NCOs are chosen for their superior performance records and exemplary legal, moral, and ethical standards. It is essential that those who continue to excel in these demanding roles are given strong consideration for promotion, as their achievements directly reflect the highest standards of CMF 38.', '73225626d5165c73214ec207309ac6d8333e30512f0fdcd4fa70bb60659610ca', 18, 18, null, 78, null, null, false, null),
  ('77604a2e-c122-5d93-8b3b-f5f2a725de36', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'training', 'E-6', null, 'MOS 38W3O Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (SSG) — Professional Military Education', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'Senior Leader Course: Obtaining recognition as the Distinguished Honor Graduate, Honor Graduate or Commandant’s List at Professional Military Education (PME)courses, or any MOS enhancing courses attended should be the goal of all CA NCOs.', '0913f3d56dba8d5b6f19cf71a61d0083169f6501b2c9cbd2b37d1f769f1fa527', 18, 18, null, 79, null, null, false, null),
  ('7aeb8c62-b21d-5c9f-be95-46dd68d9c093', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'development', 'E-6', null, 'MOS 38W3O Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (SSG) — Institutional Domain Training', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'SERE-C (High Risk), Static Line Jumpmaster Course, Ranger, Sapper Leader Course, Reconnaissance and Surveillance Leaders Course, Critical Infrastructure Course, H2F Course, Fundamentals of Global Health Engagement I, Fundamentals of Global Health Engagement II, Military Medical Humanitarian Assistance Course, Joint Special Operations Medical Orientation Course (SOC 3627) Jungle Medicine, Critical Care Paramedic, Flight Paramedic.', '25b86df79df8d3fb2e3f59c05b3a63ac1614438fcd0bc070015a0acc44e8c358', 18, 18, null, 80, null, null, false, null),
  ('b03f87e6-ec83-59fd-b7b4-dd32fb74fd82', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'development', 'E-6', null, 'MOS 38W3O Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (SSG) — Proponent note', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'The Critical Infrastructure Course is a non-ATRRS accredited course, thus a letter must be sent to the evaluation board with a certificate of completion to receive credit during the board process.', '353ac2faecddba2a121e3fc0a5a5170672c68d15ed85f71a26c800054a75826b', 18, 18, null, 81, null, null, false, null),
  ('e19ea7a3-cfc1-51d9-a561-0e256b13dd94', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'development', 'E-6', null, 'MOS 38W3O Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (SSG) — Operational Domain', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'Competitive boards/competitions, Expert Soldier/Field Medical Badge.', '3e0465c914bea098a010874e117a233406ba8a1d93b688b5d54df50672dd419f', 18, 18, null, 82, null, null, false, null),
  ('c6fc111d-6a02-50fd-8c12-4792c80022fd', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38W', null, 'promotion_criteria', 'E-6', null, 'MOS 38W3O Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (SSG) — promotion consideration', 'Chapter 4. Military Occupational Specialties Civil Reconnaissance Sergeant (38R), Civil Affairs Medical Sergeant (38W)', 'l. Proponent Guidance.
(1) Most Qualified (MQ).
(a) Displayed superior performance while serving a minimum of 36-months rated time on a Civil Affairs Team.
(b) Physical Fitness. Must meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and pass the AFT with a total score of 450 or better with a minimum of 90 points in each event in accordance with Army Directive 2025-06.
(c) Met language proficiency standards (per AR 11-6, 1-15 and USASOC Reg 350-1) by scoring 1+ on Oral Proficiency Interview (16 in Speak) or 1+/1+ on the Defense Language Proficiency Test (16 in Read and Listen) within 12-months as reflected on the Soldier Talent Profile.
(d) Must be SERE-C (High Risk) qualified.
(e) Complete one of the following courses: Static Line Jumpmaster Course, Ranger School, Sapper Leader Course, Reconnaissance and Surveillance Leaders Course.
(f) A pattern of clearly stated strong, quantifiable ratings and enumerations by the Senior Rater that denotes promote ahead of peers and displays the superior potential to serve at the next higher grade.
(2) Fully Qualified (FQ).
(a) Displayed satisfactory performance while serving a minimum of 24-months rated time on a Civil Affairs Team.
(b) Physical Fitness. Must meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and pass the AFT with a total score of 400 or better with a minimum of 80 points in each event in accordance with Army Directive 2025-06 or be on a current profile.
(c) Met language proficiency standards (per AR 11-6, 1-15 and USASOC Reg 350-1) by scoring 1+ on Oral Proficiency Interview (16 in Speak) or 1+/1+ on the Defense Language Proficiency Test (16 in Read and Listen) within 12-months as reflected on the Soldier Talent Profile.
(d) Must be SERE-C (High Risk) qualified.
(e) A pattern of clearly stated satisfactory, quantifiable ratings and enumerations by the Senior Rater that denotes promote with peers and displays potential to serve at the next higher grade.
(3) Not Fully Qualified (NFQ).
(a) Has not completed a minimum of 24-months rated time on a Civil Affairs Team.
(b) Physical Fitness. Does not meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and/or has not passed the AFT with a total score of 400 or better with a minimum of 80 points in each event in accordance with Army Directive 2025-06 or be on a current profile.
(c) Scored a 1 or less on the Oral Proficiency Interview (Speak) or Defense Language Proficiency Test (Read and Listen) within 12-months as reflected on the Soldier Talent Profile.
(d) Not SERE-C (High Risk) qualified.
(4) Not Fully Qualified for Retention (NFQ-R).
(a) The following must have occurred within the current grade and the 18 months prior to the most current evaluation board:
(1) Leadership: Received a GOMOR or conviction by court-martial in current grade or received a Bar to continued service IAW AR 601-28.
(2) Received NCOER in current grade with any of the following:
(a) Relief for Cause.
(b) Annotation of “Did Not Meet Standard” in Part IV (Performance Evaluation, Professionalism, Attributes, and Competencies), block c (Character).
(c) Annotation of “Not Qualified” in Part V (Senior Rater Overall Potential), block a (Rated Noncommissioned Officer’s Potential).
(3) Assignments: Declines to serve on a CA unit of action, directed assignment, or is unable to meet assignment requirements due to loss of security clearance, flags, or adverse actions.
(4) Military Education: Refuses to complete PME, and/or consistently fails to meet the prerequisites to attend PME or advance skill courses.
(5) Physical Fitness: Fails to score 300 points with a minimum of 60 points in each event on the AFT in accordance with Army Directive 2025-06, effective 1 June 2025, and/or fails to meet height / weight requirements IAW AR 600-9.
(a) Proponent note. Language capable ARSOF MOSs 38Z, 38R, 38W while serving as Drill Sergeants, Recruiters, and Observer Controllers are exempt from the 12-month language currency for 36 months while serving in these positions IAW Exception to Policy Memorandum dated 07 January 2020 signed by HQDA G-2 Language Team.
(b) Proponent note. Those who score L3/R3 on the DLPT or S3 on the OPI have a biennial (every 2-years) mandatory recertification requirement. Commanders must adhere to the policies identified in this chapter to re-evaluate their assigned ARSOF Soldiers’ proficiency IAW AR 11-6 dated 25 February 2022, Chapter 5-4. Army Special Operations Forces language proficiency testing.', '5510772334ffd6ed7b21167eadd60fc60981fb39a2be1c3897b2ffcecc618882', 19, 20, null, 83, null, null, false, null),
  ('9316e68c-4e75-564f-9be7-63b84fbea4aa', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'development', null, null, 'Overview', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', '5 - 1. Proponent note. Proponent guidance should not be construed as a simplified checklist for the evaluation process, nor does it guarantee selection for PME. No school or assignment is a deciding factor in the merit-based evaluation-based system. Those NCOs that have completed assignments in their Critical Leadership Developmental positions and consistently outperformed their peers, receiving most qualified rater and senior rater evaluations will be viewed favorably by board members.', 'a62d89f47a3ee0a2ce4a432a15906b101ff359657f00f0f1e0370e3b9d9ef116', 20, 20, null, 84, null, null, false, null),
  ('22eac1a1-9331-5d98-848c-063191be0a5d', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class (38Z4O) — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'The Critical Leadership Developmental position for a Sergeant First Class is CA Team Sergeant on a CAT. Without 24-months successful service as a Team Sergeant on a CAT, opportunity for promotion to Master Sergeant will be limited.', 'dc9f25d0d03b2766af1b6636bc835fb1c590425655d2b3b82a6a2c1941a5acbb', 22, 22, null, 85, null, null, false, null),
  ('e7e75e0c-1da2-5c36-8a8c-fc0be0319771', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class (38Z4O) — Proponent note', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'A Staff Sergeant may gain experience serving in a Team Sergeant position but will not receive Critical Leadership Developmental credit until after the effective date of promotion to Sergeant First Class.', '8352070ed52d36653846c23474f2382d801f132f4edd7a63922c7be353e01fd2', 22, 22, null, 86, null, null, false, null),
  ('d3cc4264-05ff-5a00-867b-123d53baf02b', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class (38Z4O) — Developmental Assignments', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'Operations NCO/Operations SGT at battalion/brigade/company, BDE Air Operations NCO/Taskings NCO/Readiness NCO/Taskings NCO/Training NCO, Medical Operations Sergeant at battalion.', 'e8a9121079bca59fc11c96a61371fe7a8c0468ce3b5e52b374130b0007e56029', 22, 22, null, 87, null, null, false, null),
  ('b5fe1ca9-9c73-57cf-a458-396d4093ced6', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class (38Z4O) — Generating Force Assignments', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'SR Instructor/Writer (Civil Affairs Assessment and Selection Senior Assessor), SWCS Senior Instructor/Writer (1st SWTG (A), 2nd SWTG (A), SWMG, NCOA), and Observer Controller/Trainer (JRTC/NTC).', '84cdd7513dc92d68d8707b2cafe1ca14e411c0b76068d1797e1281831801d908', 22, 22, null, 88, null, null, false, null),
  ('3401de94-2c70-5586-afd4-b058ccfbdb6f', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class (38Z4O) — Broadening billets and assignments', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'These include, but are not limited to, assignment at a 1st SFC (A), TSOC, GCC, USASOC, TIAD, MDEB, MDTF, Division or CORP G-9s, SFGs S-9s, and OSC or positions such as Observer Controller/Trainer (JRTC/NTC), SORB Recruiter, SOCOM USAID LNO, Inspector General (IG) NCO, Brigade Sexual Assault Response Coordinator, and Brigade Equal Opportunity Advisor.', '5db10b2363882a41254e980fe71768d918b0404a2ffe044f4f73970e6e0144df', 22, 22, null, 89, null, null, false, null),
  ('59caa0b4-dc1f-5e14-b201-f9c6f7f781c6', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class (38Z4O) — Nominative billets', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'These assignments are designated for those high performing NCOs who display a pattern of clearly stated strong, quantifiable ratings and enumerations by the Senior Rater that denotes promote ahead of peers and display outstanding potential to serve at the next higher grade. These positions include Career Management NCO (HRC PDNCO), SWCS Senior Instructor/Writer, Brigade Sexual Assault Response Coordinator or Sexual Harassment/Assault Response and Prevention Coordinator, Brigade Equal Opportunity Advisor.', 'b13c0117814ae23625c05b46b2af899fabf601c8ba95ec523a0d62a794d7e937', 22, 22, null, 90, null, null, false, null),
  ('1e89568a-0472-58cd-be9b-33a0af02fd24', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'training', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class (38Z4O) — Professional Military Education', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'Senior Leader Course: Obtaining recognition as the Distinguished Honor Graduate, Honor Graduate or Commandant’s List at Professional Military Education (PME) courses, or any MOS enhancing courses attended should be the goal of all CA NCOs.', 'd696b7eb5c7f23eec975ed3f526f89143d53c205348581f404c76e04cfb76f7b', 22, 22, null, 91, null, null, false, null),
  ('273ee0d0-5b07-5316-9cf1-ba4bd4f6e331', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class (38Z4O) — Institutional Domain Training', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'SERE-C (High Risk), Static Line Jumpmaster Course, Ranger, Sapper, Reconnaissance and Surveillance Leaders Course (RSLC), Special Warfare Network Development Course, Joint Special Operations University Planners Course, Battle Staff NCO Course, Security fundamentals course.', 'c69826df0c77978cb37df3db3bcc59c4af59713b8ae70fb9cd2d9626a96419c0', 23, 23, null, 92, null, null, false, null),
  ('569649c9-ea4b-5c18-b68c-bc27c30e642a', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class (38Z4O) — Proponent note', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', '38ZW4s Institutional Domain Training: Fundamentals of Global Health Engagement I, Fundamentals of Global Health Engagement II, Military Medical Humanitarian Assistance Course (MMHAC), Joint Special Operations Medical Orientation Course (SOC 3627).', '01d3c02b1aa65c8531b864cb888f63d5340905ab601b2ec2a85173050dcd5ea6', 23, 23, null, 93, null, null, false, null),
  ('cdb0c0a7-96aa-5a17-8dbb-918cc4197d90', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class (38Z4O) — Operational Domain', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'Competitive boards/competitions, Expert Soldier Badge/Expert Field Medical Badge.', '3b8450c8e54bc6f05456f4c569303ec54e903c33b94d35562b97b209ce41ed30', 23, 23, null, 94, null, null, false, null),
  ('5c48a179-bf1c-54c0-8a23-e15eeadde803', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'promotion_criteria', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class (38Z4O) — promotion consideration', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'm. Proponent guidance:
(1) Most Qualified (MQ).
(a) Displayed superior performance while serving a minimum of 24-months rated time as a Team Sergeant on a Civil Affairs Team.
(b) Physical Fitness. Must meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and pass the AFT with a total score of 450 or better with a minimum of 90 points in each event in accordance with Army Directive 2025-06.
(c) Met language proficiency standards (per AR 11-6, 1-15 and USASOC Reg 350-1) by scoring 1+ on Oral Proficiency Interview (16 in Speak) or 1+/1+ on the Defense Language Proficiency Test (16 in Read and Listen) within 12-months as reflected on the Soldier Talent Profile.
(d) Must be SERE-C (High Risk) qualified.
(e) Complete two of the following courses: Static Line Jumpmaster Course, Ranger School, Sapper Leader Course, Reconnaissance and Surveillance Leaders Course.
(f) A pattern of clearly stated strong, quantifiable ratings and enumerations by the Senior Rater that denotes promote ahead of peers and displays the superior potential to serve at the next higher grade.
(2) Fully Qualified (FQ).
(a) Displayed satisfactory performance while serving a minimum of 18-months rated time as a Team Sergeant on a Civil Affairs Team.
(b) Physical Fitness. Must meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and FQ: pass the AFT with a total score of 400 or better with a minimum of 80 points in each event in accordance with Army Directive 2025-06 or be on a current profile.
(c) Met language proficiency standards (per AR 11-6, 1-15 and USASOC Reg 350-1) by scoring 1+ on Oral Proficiency Interview (16 in Speak) or 1+/1+ on the Defense Language Proficiency Test (16 in Read and Listen) within 12-months as reflected on the Soldier Talent Profile.
(d) Must be SERE-C (High Risk) qualified.
(e) A pattern of clearly stated satisfactory, quantifiable ratings and enumerations by the Senior Rater that denotes promote with peers and displays potential to serve at the next higher grade.
(3) Not Fully Qualified (NFQ).
(a) Has not completed a minimum of 18-months rated time as a Team Sergeant on a Civil Affairs Team.
(b) Physical Fitness. Does not meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and/or has not passed the AFT with a total score of 400 or better with a minimum of 80 points in each event in accordance with Army Directive 2025-06 or be on a current profile.
(c) Scored a 1 or less on the Oral Proficiency Interview (Speak) or Defense Language Proficiency Test (Read and Listen) within 12-months as reflected on the Soldier Talent Profile.
(d) Not SERE-C (High Risk) qualified.
(4) Not Fully Qualified for Retention (NFQ-R).
(a) The following must have occurred within the current grade and the 18 months prior to the most current evaluation board:
(1) Leadership: Received a GOMOR or conviction by court-martial in current grade or received a Bar to continued service IAW AR 601-28.
(2) Received NCOER in current grade with any of the following:
(a) Relief for Cause.
(b) Annotation of “Did Not Meet Standard” in Part IV (Performance Evaluation, Professionalism, Attributes, and Competencies), block c (Character).
(c) Annotation of “Not Qualified” in Part V (Senior Rater Overall Potential), block a (Rated Noncommissioned Officer’s Potential).
(3) Assignments: Declines to serve on a CA unit of action, directed assignment, or is unable to meet assignment requirements due to loss of security clearance, flags, or adverse actions.
(4) Military Education: Refuses to complete PME, and/or consistently fails to meet the prerequisites to attend PME or advance skill courses.
(5) Physical Fitness: Fails to score 300 points with a minimum of 60 points in each event on the AFT in accordance with Army Directive 2025-06, effective 1 June 2025, and/or fails to meet height / weight requirements IAW AR 600-9.
(b) Proponent note. Language capable ARSOF MOSs 38Z, 38R, 38W while serving as Drill Sergeants, Recruiters, and Observer Controllers are exempt from the 12-month language currency for 36 months while serving in these positions IAW Exception to Policy Memorandum dated 07 January 2020 signed by HQDA G-2 Language Team.
(c) Proponent Note: Those who score L3/R3 on the DLPT or S3 on the OPI have a biennial (every 2-years) mandatory recertification requirement. Commanders must adhere to the policies identified in this chapter to re-evaluate their assigned ARSOF Soldiers’ proficiency IAW AR 11-6 dated 25 February 2022, Chapter 5-4. Army Special Operations Forces language proficiency testing.', '6cf57ea6bbae5e2e38d105d83d0918c8c78a1766e7022c79755443255de4b05f', 23, 24, null, 95, null, null, false, null),
  ('e3182c03-c511-543a-b329-c8b73722f010', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'assignments', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant (MOS 38Z5O/38Z5M) — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'The entry-level position for Master Sergeants is Operations Sergeant. The Critical Leadership Developmental position for a Master Sergeant is First Sergeant. There is no substitute for an operational force First Sergeant. Without 24 months successful service as a First Sergeant, opportunity for promotion to Sergeant Major will be limited.', '79fb544f26e62b9843c430d8ce21c007685559b3052e1249a8c67c8815af73ff', 24, 24, null, 96, null, null, false, null),
  ('1dff62c1-cc93-563d-94b7-f317670572ff', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant (MOS 38Z5O/38Z5M) — Proponent note', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'The most competitive First Sergeants will be selected for a subsequent First Sergeant position through the Active Component First Sergeant Selection Panel on merit. First Sergeants selected for a subsequent position will be selected for either battalion/brigade HHCs, SWCS, NCOA, Special Warfare Medical Group (SWMG) (A).', '204c33e0be5ae3e6f9a1ba9389d3f1a14b0b0755b4c4c237472f20428ec19a30', 25, 25, null, 97, null, null, false, null),
  ('d2f75283-ebc1-5523-8e2f-43be63290be8', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'assignments', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant (MOS 38Z5O/38Z5M) — Developmental Assignments', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'Senior Talent Management NCO, Senior Career Management NCO, CA Training Developer/Writer NCO, CA Doctrine Developer/Writer NCO, Plans NCOIC at brigade/battalion, Brigade Civil Information Management NCO, CA Senior Enlisted Medical Advisor at brigade.', 'e7b9468f019118e3c0db28a293ab9235ad091cdaa5465bcdb4e0ee38bafee66d', 25, 25, null, 98, null, null, false, null),
  ('5c1d9a09-10ad-5248-b924-7e925415d466', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant (MOS 38Z5O/38Z5M) — Proponent note', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'The brigade and battalion OPS SGT positions should be assigned to post First Sergeants who have completed their Critical Leadership Developmental time and who have been selected to be competitive for Sergeant Major through either the CMF DA selection panel or the MSG Evaluation Board for future slating.', '529d7b13b3d8b749b9f4bbb5351a4f7e568a593568edcdf0ce450a8cb91c9f10', 25, 25, null, 99, null, null, false, null),
  ('a3a2b143-0af5-55f5-8ae0-8bddf6208c08', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'assignments', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant (MOS 38Z5O/38Z5M) — Generating Force Assignments', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'Civil Affairs Assessment and Selection NCOIC, SWCS First Sergeant, SWCS Chief Instructor/Writer, Chief MLC Instructor.', 'bbc28609056afde44376cb0cf9eae28c012955c4c7e37791b2d7db75bb77e12a', 25, 25, null, 100, null, null, false, null),
  ('96f77cd4-0f5c-51f9-a17a-0ba9f8811a07', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'assignments', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant (MOS 38Z5O/38Z5M) — Broadening billets and assignments', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'These include but are not limited to assignment at 1st SFC (A). TSOCs, GCCs, USASOC, Division or CORP G-9s or positions such as Observer Controller/Trainer (JRTC/NTC).', 'cec046ef3df33458e3f2c47ed3c9452c52124709c72b3a571703c1524432bb8c', 25, 25, null, 101, null, null, false, null),
  ('36d7c79b-6c82-52e2-be7d-42e7d578f618', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'assignments', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant (MOS 38Z5O/38Z5M) — Nominative billets', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'These assignments are designated for those high performing NCOs who display a pattern of clearly stated strong, quantifiable ratings and enumerations by the Senior Rater that denotes promote ahead of peers and display outstanding potential to serve at the next higher grade. These positions include Senior Talent Management NCO (HRC PDNCO), CMF 38 Sr. Career Management NCO (CA Personnel Proponent SWCS), Civil Affairs Assessment and Selection NCOIC, Chief MLC Instructor, Chief SLC Instructor/Writer.', 'dc5c34d978efe4904ff228d99f69d74372c0b3e481b68ae730dd3b62633ad1aa', 25, 25, null, 102, null, null, false, null),
  ('a1d18641-f5d9-5386-af5c-9ce936db9931', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'training', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant (MOS 38Z5O/38Z5M) — Professional Military Education', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'Joint Special Operations Forces Senior Enlisted Academy (JSOFSEA) or the United States Sergeant Major Academy (USASMA), Obtaining recognition as the Distinguished Honor Graduate, Honor Graduate or Commandant’s List at Professional Military Education (PME) courses, or any MOS enhancing courses attended should be the goal of all CA NCOs.', '62703041bfde5d63def7a1e9335068931ed95ef7f5373e76810912d35b7077de', 25, 25, null, 103, null, null, false, null),
  ('91366f1a-e98b-5fce-9098-60a1c64ea18f', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant (MOS 38Z5O/38Z5M) — Institutional Domain Training', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'How the Army Runs Course, Active Component Leaders Orientation Course or Pre-Command Course equivalent, Special Operations Campaign Artistry Program.', '36bf2a69d53f33c3e218041e27ca4983beaf2d0618aa66fc3832cc5c29048bb1', 25, 25, null, 104, null, null, false, null),
  ('6157bf14-5ed6-54c0-80de-51dca57eeac9', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'promotion_criteria', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant/First Sergeant (MOS 38Z5O/38Z5M) — promotion consideration', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'm. Proponent guidance:
(1) Most Qualified (MQ).
(a) Displayed superior performance while serving a minimum of 24-months rated time as a First Sergeant. The Most Qualified Master Sergeants must have served as a First Sergeant in both a tactical CA company and an HHC, NCOA, or SWCS.
(b) Physical Fitness. Must meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and pass the AFT with a total score of 450 or better with a minimum of 90 points in each event in accordance with Army Directive 2025-06.
(c) Must be language current. Should meet the language proficiency standard (per AR 11-6, 1- 15 m and USASOC Reg 350-1, 4-5 a) by scoring 1+ on the Oral Proficiency Interview (16 in Speaking) or 1+/1+ on the Defense Language Proficiency Test (16 in Reading and 16 in Listening) within 12 months as reflected on the Soldier Talent Profile.
(d) Must be Static Line Jumpmaster and SERE-C (High Risk) qualified.
(e) A pattern of clearly stated strong, quantifiable ratings and enumerations by the Senior Rater that denotes promote ahead of peers and displays the superior potential to serve at the next higher grade.
(2) Fully Qualified (FQ).
(a) Displayed satisfactory performance while serving a minimum of 18-months rated time as a First Sergeant.
(b) Physical Fitness. Must meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and pass the AFT with a total score of 400 or better with a minimum of 80 points in each event in accordance with Army Directive 2025-06 or be on a current profile.
(c) Must be language current. Should meet the language proficiency standard (per AR 11-6, 1- 15 m and USASOC Reg 350-1, 4-5 a) by scoring 1+ on the Oral Proficiency Interview (16 in Speaking) or 1+/1+ on the Defense Language Proficiency Test (16 in Reading and 16 in Listening) within 12 months as reflected on the Soldier Talent Profile.
(d) Must be Static Line Jumpmaster and SERE-C (High Risk) qualified.
(e) A pattern of clearly stated satisfactory, quantifiable ratings and enumerations by the Senior Rater that denotes promote with peers and displays potential to serve at the next higher grade.
(3) Not Fully Qualified (NFQ).
(a) Has not completed minimum of 18-months rated time as a First Sergeant
(b) Physical Fitness. Does not meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 or has not passed the AFT with a total score of 400 or better with a minimum of 80 points in each event in accordance with Army Directive 2025-06 or be on a current profile.
(c) Failed to test on the Oral Proficiency Interview (Speak) or Defense Language Proficiency Test (Read and Listen) within 12 months as reflected on the Soldier Talent Profile.
(d) Not SERE-C (High Risk) qualified.
(e) Not Static Line Jumpmaster.
(4) Not Fully Qualified for Retention (NFQ-R).
(a) The following must have occurred within the current grade and the 18 months prior to the most current evaluation board:
(1) Leadership: Received a GOMOR or conviction by court-martial in current grade or received a Bar to continued service IAW AR 601-28.
(2) Received NCOER in current grade with any of the following:
(a) Relief for Cause.
(b) Annotation of “Did Not Meet Standard” in Part IV (Performance Evaluation, Professionalism, Attributes, and Competencies), block c (Character).
(c) Annotation of “Not Qualified” in Part V (Senior Rater Overall Potential), block a (Rated Noncommissioned Officer’s Potential).
(3) Assignments: Declines to serve on a CA unit of action, directed assignment, or is unable to meet assignment requirements due to loss of security clearance, flags, or adverse actions.
(4) Military Education: Refuses to complete PME, and/or consistently fails to meet the prerequisites to attend PME or advance skill courses.
(5) Physical Fitness: Fails to score 300 points with a minimum of 60 points in each event on the AFT in accordance with Army Directive 2025-06, effective 1 June 2025, and/or fails to meet height / weight requirements IAW AR 600-9.
(b) Proponent note. Language capable ARSOF MOSs 38Z, 38R, 38W while serving as Drill Sergeants, Recruiters, and Observer Controllers are exempt from the 12-month language currency for 36 months while serving in these positions IAW Exception to Policy Memorandum dated 07 January 2020 signed by HQDA G-2 Language Team.
(c) Proponent note. Those who score L3/R3 on the DLPT or S3 on the OPI have a biennial (every 2-years) mandatory recertification requirement. Commanders must adhere to the policies identified in this chapter to re-evaluate their assigned ARSOF Soldiers’ proficiency IAW AR 11-6 dated 25 February 2022, Chapter 5-4. Army Special Operations Forces language proficiency testing.', 'f4c59993bfaac6de2439813c76c38607058f0aaf071a75c3f0ace00b45599d81', 26, 27, null, 105, null, null, false, null),
  ('fef336b9-0dee-53a6-972c-00bbcd090da5', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'development', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major (MOS 38Z6O) — Additional guidance', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'The Critical Leadership Developmental position for a Sergeant Major is Operations Sergeant Major in a battalion or higher echelon. Without serving as an Operations Sergeant Major in a battalion or higher echelon, opportunity for selection to serve as Command Sergeant Major will be limited. Sergeants Major serving in competitive broadening assignments at time of promotion to Sergeant Major will be assigned to CLD position to receive Critical Leadership time credit.', 'e1ed8e31634e79ae7d4fba6de62a14831e0526dede521e7f71734a2ff5e13363', 27, 27, null, 106, null, null, false, null),
  ('286e3021-0124-51c8-b85d-a76b70ff0059', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'assignments', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major (MOS 38Z6O) — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'Command Sergeant Major at brigade Professional Development Proficiency Code(7C) or battalion level Professional Development Proficiency Code (6C), Senior Operations Sergeant Major at the brigade Professional Development Proficiency Code (7S) or battalion level Professional Development Proficiency Code (6S), Senior Operations Sergeant Major at Theater Civil Affairs Planning Team, CORPS, ASCC Professional Development Proficiency Code (7S).', '7d6955cd8bcf33949f93b6fbfa58d182f126d99b961acd1fcac2a462c5199794', 27, 27, null, 107, null, null, false, null),
  ('29be1686-61de-5c56-b937-a1e597aadf63', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'development', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major (MOS 38Z6O) — Generating/Institutional/Training Assignments', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'Chief Career Management NCO Professional Development Proficiency Code (7S), Special Warfare Training Battalion Command Sergeant Major Professional Development Proficiency Code (6C), Combined Arms Center Special Operations Directorate Senior Enlisted Advisor Professional Development Proficiency Code (7S), JSOFSEA Military Instructor Professional Development Proficiency Code (7S).', '72ec9bef5ebadadd5cded97856a79ba2612965658e72358a48a477b3ba6ddf25', 27, 27, null, 108, null, null, false, null),
  ('c21c947f-eca5-55e8-84aa-3c14a3b72bf6', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'training', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major (MOS 38Z6O) — Professional Military Education', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'Nominative Leader Course (NLC), Summit Course, Obtaining recognition as the Distinguished Honor Graduate, Honor Graduate or Commandant’s List at Professional Military Education (PME) courses, or any MOS enhancing courses attended should be the goal of all CA NCOs.', '1c72102e69b7bdf059040b7e24ba91af73a86be2614d4ec82ffd3ba1b77c8987', 27, 27, null, 109, null, null, false, null),
  ('c9a0b5c2-11e4-5dac-84aa-af574c3d4a3e', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'development', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major (MOS 38Z6O) — Institutional Domain Training', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'The Battalion and Brigade Pre-Command Course (BBPCC), Command Sergeants Major Development Course, How the Army Runs Course.', '114783041b675a37043cda21e54410cfdccbcd74b291f1030daca78074a869db', 28, 28, null, 110, null, null, false, null),
  ('7e7a5184-d59d-5410-be74-f6171474f831', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38Z', null, 'promotion_criteria', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major (MOS 38Z6O) — promotion consideration', 'Chapter 5. Military Occupational Specialty 38Z, Civil Affairs Senior Sergeant', 'i. Proponent guidance:
(1) Most Qualified (MQ).
(a) Displayed superior performance while serving a minimum of 12-months rated time as a Battalion Operations Sergeant Major in a Civil Affairs Battalion. The most qualified SGMs must have served as an Operations Sergeant Major in a CA Battalion and in a SGM broadening assignment.
(b) Physical Fitness. Must meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and pass the AFT with a total score of 450 or better with a minimum of 90 points in each event in accordance with Army Directive 2025-06.
(c) Must be language current. Should meet the language proficiency standard (per AR 11-6, 1- 15 m and USASOC Reg 350-1, 4-5 a) by scoring 1+ on the Oral Proficiency Interview (16 in Speaking) or 1+/1+ on the Defense Language Proficiency Test (16 in Reading and 16 in Listening) within 12 months as reflected on the Soldier Talent Profile.
(d) Must be Jumpmaster and SERE-C (High Risk) qualified.
(e) A pattern of clearly stated strong, quantifiable ratings and enumerations by the Senior Rater that denotes promote ahead of peers and displays the superior potential to serve at the next higher grade.
(2) Fully Qualified (FQ).
(a) Displayed outstanding performance while serving a minimum of 12-months rated time as a Battalion Operations Sergeant Major in a Civil Affairs battalion.
(b) Physical Fitness. Must meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and pass the AFT with a total score of 400 or better with a minimum of 80 points in each event in accordance with Army Directive 2025-06 or be on a current profile.
(c) Must be language current. Should meet the language proficiency standard (per AR 11-6, 1- 15 m and USASOC Reg 350-1, 4-5 a) by scoring 1+ on the Oral Proficiency Interview (16 in Speaking) or 1+/1+ on the Defense Language Proficiency Test (16 in Reading and 16 in Listening) within 12 months as reflected on the Soldier Talent Profile.
(d) Must be Jumpmaster and SERE-C (High Risk) qualified.
(e) A pattern of clearly stated satisfactory, quantifiable ratings and enumerations by the Senior Rater that denotes promote with peers and displays potential to serve at the next higher grade.
(3) Not Fully Qualified (NFQ).
(a) Has not completed a minimum of 12-months rated time as a Battalion Operations Sergeant Major in a Civil Affairs battalion.
(b) Physical Fitness. Does not meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and/or has not passed the AFT with a total score of 400 or better with a minimum of 80 points in each event in accordance with Army Directive 2025-06 or be on a current profile.
(c) Failed to test on the Oral Proficiency Interview (Speak) or Defense Language Proficiency Test (Read and Listen) within 12 months as reflected on the Soldier Talent Profile.
(d) Not SERE-C (High Risk) qualified.
(e) Not Static Line Jumpmaster
(4) Not Fully Qualified for Retention (NFQ-R).
(a) The following must have occurred within the current grade and the 18 months prior to the most current evaluation board:
(1) Leadership: Received a GOMOR or conviction by court-martial in current grade or received a Bar to continued service IAW AR 601-28.
(2) Received NCOER in current grade with any of the following:
(a) Relief for Cause.
(b) Annotation of “Did Not Meet Standard” in Part IV (Performance Evaluation, Professionalism, Attributes, and Competencies), block c (Character).
(c) Annotation of “Not Qualified” in Part V (Senior Rater Overall Potential), block a (Rated Noncommissioned Officer’s Potential).
(3) Assignments: Declines to serve on a CA unit of action, directed assignment, or is unable to meet assignment requirements due to loss of security clearance, flags, or adverse actions.
(4) Military Education: Refuses to complete PME, and/or consistently fails to meet the prerequisites to attend PME or advance skill courses.
(5) Physical Fitness: Fails to score 300 points with a minimum of 60 points in each event on the AFT in accordance with Army Directive 2025-06, effective 1 June 2025, and/or fails to meet height / weight requirements IAW AR 600-9.
(b) Proponent note. Language capable ARSOF MOSs 38Z, 38R, 38W while serving as Drill Sergeants, Recruiters, and Observer Controllers are exempt from the 12-month language currency for 36 months while serving in these positions IAW Exception to Policy Memorandum dated 07 January 2020 signed by HQDA G-2 Language Team.
(c) Proponent note. Those who score L3/R3 on the DLPT or S3 on the OPI have a biennial (every 2-years) mandatory recertification requirement. Commanders must adhere to the policies identified in this chapter to re-evaluate their assigned ARSOF Soldiers’ proficiency IAW AR 11-6 dated 25 February 2022, Chapter 5-4. Army Special Operations Forces language proficiency testing.', '3e82591a36b9b6194fc3ba32ccdc7014c2c97329b7314c404ef2f8aa1d15301c', 28, 29, null, 111, null, null, false, null),
  ('08c19999-ee12-518a-b5b1-c20a19ef5935', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'development', null, null, 'Overview', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', '6 - 1. Proponent Note. The USAR Civil Affairs structure comprises Troop Program Unit (TPU) and Active Guard and Reserve (AGR) Soldiers. TPU Soldiers typically participate in monthly Battle Assemblies and up to 29 days of Annual Training per year. The AGR force provides full- time support to the Reserve, encompassing training, administration, maintenance, readiness, mobilization, and deployment.
a. All USAR Civil Affairs Soldiers must cultivate expertise in: Civil Affairs Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures (TTPs) Combined arms operations across the competition continuum (competition, conflict, crisis) with a focus on Large-Scale Combat Operations (LSCO) Stability, civil-military, and security cooperation activities supporting Irregular Warfare (IW) Targeting and mission planning processes Support and sustainment processes Joint, Interagency, Intergovernmental, and Multinational (JIIM) aspects of Civil Affairs Operations (CAO).
b. AGR NCOs serve in Title 10 status to meet the full-time support and readiness requirements for USAR projects, programs and missions. AGR 38B NCOs organize, administer, instruct, and train the Reserve Component. In addition to the professional development steps and standards for career progression AGR NCOs require unique knowledge, skills, and training to perform their full-time operational & support duties.', '4610dbc3763004861c90d577a38dc75935e7f4c7cb19178c3a198353a962a4d6', 30, 30, null, 112, null, null, false, null),
  ('797558e6-0b12-500f-b730-55590dfdd868', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'assignments', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant (38B2O) — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', '(1) The Critical Leadership Developmental position for a Troop Program Unit Sergeant is serving as a Civil Affairs Sergeant on a Civil Affairs Planning Team (CAPT), an Operations Sergeant in a company Headquarters, or a CA Sergeant in a CMOC and Civil Liaison Team in a Brigade HHC. Without an assignment in one of these positions the opportunity for promotion to Staff Sergeant will be limited.
(2) The Critical Leadership Development position for AGR Sergeant is as a CA Sergeant in a CA brigade HHC or Civil Affairs Command.', 'b1c46e379709c36e2ae86d5329ed56577ca821f38193f1590c7ee861f3256c8e', 32, 32, null, 113, null, null, false, null),
  ('aac36da6-3f77-5b11-af97-62aae11c681c', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'assignments', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant (38B2O) — Generating Force Assignments', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'Civil Affairs NCO, Recruiter (AGR Only).', '381d21d00859a00de15d5fb67d3487a4ef7d728d100966e148eb17e579cac0e9', 32, 32, null, 114, null, null, false, null),
  ('e1910145-690e-5ca8-a4e4-247fbb7ef660', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'development', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant (38B2O) — Proponent note', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'Sergeants should serve in Operational Force at their current grade for at least 24 months before being assigned to the Generating/Institutional/Training Force.', 'bd0d85275e6a243be143a950be598c037e8dc38dae16459bbdb9f457b1af24e5', 32, 32, null, 115, null, null, false, null),
  ('8da21c88-94a1-533e-96b7-d2e18de54a8b', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'training', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant (38B2O) — Professional Military Education', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'Basic Leaders Course.', '0144a0702da63efc6cb90b59b5927933230882689f8e3683e6d66131219e9cde', 32, 32, null, 116, null, null, false, null),
  ('d5c9cac5-b903-5593-af23-78f5309a7b9c', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'training', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant (38B2O) — Functional Training', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'Master Fitness Trainer Course, Static Line Jumpmaster course (for airborne assignments) and other ASI/SQI Producing Courses. USAR Soldiers should also pursue job specific and functional courses that enhance professional technical skills and knowledge at various schools including the Army Reserve Readiness Training Center (ARRTC).', '6aec7735c3662238edd870afe3ca441a11f0ec1d66180aa7dfc8726f35f592f8', 32, 32, null, 117, null, null, false, null),
  ('bcff971b-049a-5abc-afe2-cad27f469146', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'lifelong_learning', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant (38B2O) — Lifelong Learning', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'Army Continuing Education System, Army Correspondence Course Program, Army University, Defense Activity for Non-Traditional Education Support, Army IgnitED, Joint Service Transcript, working toward an associate degree.', '73ed5463d0728232c9b3fffd4782820d6fdd5ec9afedfe9c2e167795121beded', 32, 32, null, 118, null, null, false, null),
  ('aedae819-cd72-5695-8c72-e56cf40fc749', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'development', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant (38B2O) — Proponent guidance', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'Selection for promotion is based on a Soldier’s potential to serve at levels of increasing responsibility, stemming from a leader’s assessment of a Soldier in his or her current rank. Special consideration should be given to those SGTs serving in SSG Positions, consistently selected as the Distinguished Honor Graduate, Honor Graduate, Top 20% Commandant''s List, or other honors during NCOPDS or MOS enhancing courses. Has been awarded the Expert Soldier Badge.', '01fde182d1f3a50b20a0c0badd1b7f571e9590b3cc2121905f8806566a4baa35', 32, 32, null, 119, null, null, false, null),
  ('913bd4a8-e62b-5400-b15d-55acc9ad1447', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant (38B3O) — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', '(1) The Critical Leadership Developmental position for a TPU Staff Sergeant is serving as a CA NCO on a Civil Affairs Team, Civil Affairs Planning Team or Civil Liaison Team, or as an instructor.
(2) The Critical Leadership Development position for an Active Guard Reserve (AGR) Staff Sergeant is Drill Sergeant or CA NCO at the CACOM, Brigade HHC, or Battalion HHC.', '6d4147779dc402018400463941cb2e82a96c71a0d7bd9cdcbb98eebee8f56e03', 33, 33, null, 120, null, null, false, null),
  ('07c7a5df-7d0c-565a-9910-d41dbf38f9ae', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant (38B3O) — Developmental Assignments', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'CA/Assistant Training NCO, Air NCO, Civil Affairs CA Planner, CA NCO, CA TM SGT, and TASS BN Positions.', 'b5e369e18347166cfb880cab78230a7888b77702316c949b575cba1a585260df', 33, 33, null, 121, null, null, false, null),
  ('143aa7ba-5d01-50eb-8fe5-e537edaeafc1', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant (38B3O) — Generating Force Assignments', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'Drill Sergeant (AGR Only), Instructor, CA Plans NCO (AGR Only), Recruiter (AGR Only).', 'ec9d521bb4f7c68e2cabd9dcbfa4bfcc98d08a751ba62a61b33fa047627a4265', 33, 33, null, 122, null, null, false, null),
  ('4184ceb6-7158-563c-9a4f-a56074b5e39b', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'development', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant (38B3O) — Proponent note', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'Staff Sergeants should serve in Operational Force at their current grade for at least 24 months before being assigned to the Generating/Institutional/Training Force. Special consideration should be given to those AGR SSGs serving in the Drill Sergeant Positions and/or earning the basic instructor badge, SSGs who are selected as the DHG, HG, Top 20% Commandant’s list, or other honors during NCOPDS or MOS enhancing courses.', 'e9f6a000c8e88a457458c438d01c55db82a671659ab568640a4922ddea7cfb76', 33, 33, null, 123, null, null, false, null),
  ('16609f7d-8b35-5bb9-a848-696a5d873a31', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'training', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant (38B3O) — Professional Military Education', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'Advanced Leader Course.', 'a166250a572240b3d1f79b0351e70a8d0b57b64ddfa9bccdabb64b953bf12d1d', 33, 33, null, 124, null, null, false, null),
  ('bcb9554d-1e4e-50bc-af7e-48dea36659e7', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'training', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant (38B3O) — Functional Training', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'Battle Staff Noncommissioned Officer Course, Master Resiliency Trainer Course, Master Fitness Trainer Course, Static Line Jumpmaster course (for airborne assignments), and other ASI/SQI Producing Courses. USAR Soldiers should also pursue job specific and functional courses that enhance professional technical skills and knowledge at various schools including the Army Reserve Readiness Training Center (ARRTC).', '0ae99cfff85ca86559e53c54f19a62e435f05d4e41fa4147632817d69678b12f', 33, 33, null, 125, null, null, false, null),
  ('67a1a8c2-ba34-5453-8f74-86ed70bcaa7d', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'lifelong_learning', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant (38B3O) — Lifelong Learning', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'Army Continuing Education System, Army Correspondence Course Program, Army University, Defense Activity for Non-Traditional Education Support, Army IgnitED, Joint Service Transcript, working toward associate degree. Training at the Center for Excellence in Disaster Management and Humanitarian Assistance is also encouraged. These courses include but are not limited to: Humanitarian Assistance Response Training, Health Emergencies in Large Populations, Health and Humanitarian Action in Emergencies.', '50771bea5d001fed16792de324a204c6e5a4b6bd38121497c4558cd70f60dcda', 33, 33, null, 126, null, null, false, null),
  ('1e208962-f0c1-5153-88ea-6ec1ea1acef8', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'promotion_criteria', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant (38B3O) — promotion consideration', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'j. Proponent guidance:
(1) Most Qualified (MQ).
(a) TPU Staff Sergeants that displayed superior performance while serving a minimum of 24- months rated time as a CA NCO on a Civil Affairs Team or Civil Liaison Team. AGR Staff Sergeants that displayed superior performance while serving a minimum of 24-months rated time as a Drill Sergeant, Instructor or as a CA NCO at the Brigade HHC, or Battalion HHC.
(b) Physical Fitness. Must meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and pass the AFT with a total score of 450 or better with a minimum of 90 points in each event IAW Army Directive 2025-06.
(c) A pattern of clearly stated strong, quantifiable ratings and enumerations by the Senior Rater that denotes promote ahead of peers and displays the superior potential to serve at the next higher grade.
(d) Complete two of the following courses: Battle Staff NCO course, ATRRS Operator, USAR Training Plan Development, USAR Training Manager, USAR Unit Mobilization Planner.
(e) Language skills are not a requirement but are highly encouraged and will place Soldiers above their peers. A FLPR of 1+/1+ or higher should be viewed as exceptional. A FLPR of 2/2 or higher should be viewed as most qualifying.
(f) Civilian Education: completion of an associate’s degree or attained 60 credit hours towards a bachelor’s degree, or multiple civilian technical skills certifications viewed as exceptional. Completion of a bachelor’s, graduate, or post-graduate degree should be viewed as most qualifying.
(2) Fully Qualified (FQ).
(a) TPU Staff Sergeants that met satisfactory performance standards while serving a minimum of 24-months rated time as a Drill Sergeant, CA AIT Instructor or a CA NCO on a Civil Affairs Team or Civil Liaison Team. AGR Staff Sergeants that displayed outstanding performance while serving a minimum of 24-months rated time as a Drill Sergeant, Instructor or CA NCO at CACOM/brigade level.
(b) Physical Fitness. Must meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and pass the AFT with a total score of 300 or better with a minimum of 60 points in each event IAW Army Directive 2025-06 or be on a current profile.
(c) A pattern of clearly stated satisfactory, quantifiable ratings and enumerations by the Senior Rater that denotes promote with peers and displays potential to serve at the next higher grade.
(d) language skills are not a requirement but are highly encouraged and will place Soldiers above their peers. FLPR of 1/1 or higher should be viewed as highly qualifying.
(e) Civilian education: completion of 45 or more credit hours towards a college degree or a civilian technical certification.
(3) Not Fully Qualified (NFQ).
(a) Troop Program Unit Staff Sergeants that displayed below average performance while serving a minimum of 24-months rated time as a CA NCO on a Civil Affairs Team or Civil Liaison Team or those having not served the 24 months minimum duration in the position while having met time in grade requirements. AGR Staff Sergeants that displayed average (adequate) performance while serving a minimum of 24-months rated time as a Drill Sergeant, Instructor/Writer, Instructor, or CA NCO at CACOM/brigade level or those having not served the 24-months minimum duration in the position while having met time in grade requirements.
(b) Physical Fitness. Does not meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and/or has not passed the AFT with a total score of 300 or better with a minimum of 60 points in each event IAW Army Directive 2025-06 or be on a current profile.
(4) Not Fully Qualified for Retention (NFQ-R).
(a)The following must have occurred within the current grade and the 18 months prior to the most current evaluation board:
(1) Leadership: Received a GOMOR or conviction by court-martial in current grade or received a Bar to continued service IAW AR 601-28.
(2) Received NCOER in current grade with any of the following:
(a) Relief for Cause.
(b) Annotation of “Did Not Meet Standard” in Part IV (Performance Evaluation, Professionalism, Attributes, and Competencies), block c (Character).
(c) Annotation of “Not Qualified” in Part V (Senior Rater Overall Potential), block a (Rated Noncommissioned Officer’s Potential).
(3) Assignments: Declines to serve on a CA unit of action, directed assignment, or is unable to meet assignment requirements due to loss of security clearance, flags, or adverse actions.
(4) Military Education: Refuses to complete PME, and/or consistently fails to meet the prerequisites to attend PME or advance skill courses.
(5) Physical Fitness: Fails to score 300 points with a minimum of 60 points in each event on the AFT IAW Army Directive 2025-06, effective 1 June 2025, and/or fails to meet height / weight requirements IAW AR 600-9.', '5c00a6506878860da037fcd75dae104923675dfff3de7c62d042ba2d5fa7b0f9', 34, 35, null, 127, null, null, false, null),
  ('3468a3cf-8f86-5c5a-b41d-43d8de30351c', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class (38B4O) — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', '(1) The Critical Leadership Development position for a TPU Sergeant First Class is serving as a Team Sergeant on a CAT. Without an assignment as a Team Sergeant on a CAT, opportunity for promotion to Master Sergeant will be limited.
(2) The Critical Leadership Development positions for an AGR Sergeant First Class in the operational force are Operations NCO, J-9 NCOIC, CA Team Sergeant, CA Plans NCO, or Air Team Leader.
(3) Proponent note. To gain valuable operational experience, it is recommended that Troop Program Unit (TPU) Sergeants First Class serve in Operational Force assignments at their current grade before transitioning to Generating/Institutional/Training Force roles. However, recognizing the limited opportunities for Sergeants First Class to gain Operational Force experience at their current grade within the Active Guard and Reserve (AGR) structure, prior operational experience at lower grades is acceptable and should not disqualify candidates for AGR positions. When considering AGR Sergeants First Class for positions, prioritize those with experience in both operational and generating force assignments. Examples of relevant roles include Senior Drill Sergeant or Operations Sergeant (A Co, 5th BN, 1st SWTG(A)), Instructor/Writer (D Co, 5th BN, 3rd BN, 1st SWTG(A)), Operations NCO (SWCS HHC), CA Plans NCO (PACOM), Air Team Leader (USACAPOC), J-9 NCOIC (EUCOM).', '6b97655131d775bece6ee3789a137b63615ae8201379c836e62dea37d8ca1656', 35, 35, null, 128, null, null, false, null),
  ('ebf6835f-40ac-599d-92b9-5a6c0ee96db8', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class (38B4O) — Developmental Assignments', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'CA Noncommissioned Officer (TM LDR), Training Noncommissioned Officer, Air Noncommissioned Officer, Operations Noncommissioned Officer, CA Plans Noncommissioned Officer, Training Sergeant, J9 NCOIC CIV-MIL Integrator and Civil Affairs NCO, Civil Affairs Sergeant.', '4046b95962f7ff25c3330ab7b10d5926b18027451a06496af5b82b742140198e', 35, 35, null, 129, null, null, false, null),
  ('885850c8-e59a-5056-873a-043a80ec1233', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class (38B4O) — Generating Force Assignments', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'Senior Drill Sergeant, Observer Controller/Trainer, Senior Instructor, Instructor, Senior Instructor/Writer, CA Assistant Platoon Sergeant, Training Noncommissioned Officer, or Operations Noncommissioned Officer. Most AGR Critical Leadership Development positions for Sergeants First Class exist as an Instructor, Training Noncommissioned Officer, Operations Noncommissioned Officer, as well as other positions listed above.', 'fd2c34c5b79af2ae3c4dae7fade69c6090a2e16524ffc63e0cb9d1c80d440075', 36, 36, null, 130, null, null, false, null),
  ('9fc140a7-4185-5809-b77e-b2795095b23b', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'training', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class (38B4O) — Professional Military Education', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'Master Leaders Course.', '461889493bec7ab8256ef28730535e0adbfe6f15fe623b5c052ac25d040cc21a', 36, 36, null, 131, null, null, false, null),
  ('cff6ddbe-419e-5d35-9c5d-b78c4e54cf88', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'training', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class (38B4O) — Functional Training', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'Battle Staff NCO Course, H2F Integrator Course, Static Line Jumpmaster course (for airborne assignments), and other ASI/SQI Producing Courses. USAR Soldiers should also pursue job specific and functional courses that enhance professional technical skills and knowledge at various schools including the Army Reserve Readiness Training Center (ARRTC), Network Engagement, Analysis and Targeting.', '89cadec597569a4bf786e5f0c75f03b9dd49a17a436dc90270283e0f2d9a01a1', 36, 36, null, 132, null, null, false, null),
  ('c955effd-3d9b-55f4-95ed-096b4a987fea', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'lifelong_learning', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class (38B4O) — Lifelong Learning', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'Army Continuing Education System, Army Correspondence Course Program Army University, Defense Activity for Non-Traditional Education Support, Army IgnitED, Joint Service Transcript, completed an associate degree or equivalent credits, working toward a bachelor’s degree. Training at the Center for Excellence in Disaster Management and Humanitarian Assistance is also encouraged. These courses include but are not limited to: Humanitarian Assistance Response Training, Health Emergencies in Large Populations, Health and Humanitarian Action in Emergencies.', 'e673f64d73b750ea3e159bcac7cdf2d95b4a8efc17ee7630a123628218448d53', 36, 36, null, 133, null, null, false, null),
  ('7e38c879-9bc4-54dc-b90e-cb32cea5924c', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'promotion_criteria', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class (38B4O) — promotion consideration', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'i. Proponent guidance:
(1) Most Qualified (MQ).
(a) TPU Sergeants First Class that displayed superior performance while serving 24 months as a Team Sergeant on a Civil Affairs Team. AGR Sergeants First Class that displayed superior performance while serving 24 months in a valid 38B4 position with special consideration being given to those serving or having previously served in the Senior Drill Sergeant position.
(b) Physical Fitness: Must meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and pass the AFT with a total score of 450 or better with a minimum of 90 points in each event IAW Army Directive 2025-06.
(c) A pattern of clearly stated strong, quantifiable ratings and enumerations by the Senior Rater that denotes promote ahead of peers and displays outstanding potential to serve at the next higher grade.
(d) Complete two of the following courses: Battle Staff NCO course, ATRRS Operator, USAR Training Plan Development, USAR Training Manager, USAR Unit Mobilization Planner, USAR Security Manager, USAR Physical Security Officer, USAR Quota Manager.
(e) Awarded Drill Sergeant of the cycle or Instructor of the cycle. SFCs who are selected as the DHG, HG, Top 20% Commandant’s list, or other honors during NCOPDS or MOS enhancing courses.
(f) Language skills are not a requirement but are highly encouraged and will place Soldiers above their peers. A FLPR of 1+/1+ or higher should be viewed as exceptional. A FLPR of 2/2 or higher should be viewed as most qualifying.
(g) Civilian Education: completion of an associate’s degree or attained 60 credit hours towards a bachelor’s degree, or multiple civilian technical skills certifications viewed as exceptional. Completion of a bachelor’s, graduate, or post-graduate degree should be viewed as most qualifying.
(2) Fully Qualified (FQ).
(a) TPU Sergeants First Class that met satisfactory performance standards while serving 24 months as a Team Sergeant on a Civil Affairs Team. AGR Sergeants First Class that displayed satisfactory performance for 24 months as a Senior Drill Sergeant, Senior Instructor/Writer, or Training Noncommissioned Officer.
(b) Physical Fitness. Must meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and pass the AFT with a total score of 300 or better with a minimum of 60 points in each event IAW Army Directive 2025-06 or be on a current profile.
(c) A pattern of clearly stated satisfactory quantifiable ratings and enumerations by the Senior Rater that denotes promote with peers and displays potential to serve at the next higher grade.
(3) Not Fully Qualified (NFQ).
(a) TPU Sergeants First Class that displayed below average performance while serving 24 months as a Team Sergeant on a Civil Affairs Team. AGR Sergeants First Class that displayed average performance for 24 months as a Senior Drill Sergeant, Senior Instructor/Writer, or Training Noncommissioned Officer.
(b) Physical Fitness. Does not meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and/or has not passed the AFT with a total score of 300 or better with a minimum of 60 points in each event IAW Army Directive 2025-06 or be on a current profile.
(c) A clear articulation by the senior rater that denotes to only promote if there is a requirement with average quantifiable ratings and enumerations. Displays average potential to serve at the next higher grade.
(4) Not Fully Qualified for Retention (NFQ-R).
(a) The following must have occurred within the current grade and the 18 months prior to the most current evaluation board:
(1) Leadership: Received a GOMOR or conviction by court-martial in current grade or received a Bar to continued service IAW AR 601-28.
(2) Received NCOER in current grade with any of the following:
(a) Relief for Cause.
(b) Annotation of “Did Not Meet Standard” in Part IV (Performance Evaluation, Professionalism, Attributes, and Competencies), block c (Character).
(c) Annotation of “Not Qualified” in Part V (Senior Rater Overall Potential), block a (Rated Noncommissioned Officer’s Potential).
(3) Assignments: Declines to serve on a CA unit of action, directed assignment, or is unable to meet assignment requirements due to loss of security clearance, flags, or adverse actions.
(4) Military Education: Refuses to complete PME, and/or consistently fails to meet the prerequisites to attend PME or advance skill courses.
(5) Physical Fitness: Fails to score 300 points with a minimum of 60 points in each event on the AFT IAW Army Directive 2025-06, effective 1 June 2025, and/or fails to meet height / weight requirements IAW AR 600-9.', '52c4ee54593e6150946d3180d0c08b9653f0db427404998e6dca28369d0907ba', 36, 37, null, 134, null, null, false, null),
  ('5f64406b-3859-5a2a-9a8b-02aa90b8b4ad', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'assignments', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant (38B5O) — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', '(1) The Critical Leadership Development position for the Troop Program Unit Master Sergeant is serving as a First Sergeant.
(2) The entry-level position for AGR Master Sergeants is Team Sergeant of a Civil Affairs Planning Team in a CA battalion. Without 24-months rated time at the battalion level opportunities for advancement will be limited. The Critical Leadership Developmental positions for the AGR Master Sergeant are USAR First Sergeant Board selected, First Sergeant serving in SWCS, CA Sergeant in a CA BDE, and CA NCO at a CACOM. Assignment at the CACOM level should be limited to those MSGs who have successfully served at a BDE level for a minimum of 24 months. Without 24-months rated time in a Critical Leadership Development position, opportunity for attendance at U.S. Army Sergeants Major Course will be limited.', 'fe48ab2b9936719968ce394051eda0b66f84b07323e75338f6632974a4e8e961', 38, 38, null, 135, null, null, false, null),
  ('a17139c5-db66-58b8-b152-aae625f02477', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'assignments', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant (38B5O) — Developmental Assignments', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'CA/Senior Operations NCO, Senior Plans NCO, Senior Training NCO, MIL-to-MIL Program Manager, Joint USAFRICOM assignment (J5) Senior Enlisted Reserve Liaison. Post BDE/CACOM assignment. Public Health NCO, Civil Affairs NCO (COMPO 2-specific), CA Operations Sergeant (COMPO 2-specific).', 'a28bb128e22d5050834a439fb38eaadd62b1253b126fc4c8e11caee90e70def1', 38, 38, null, 136, null, null, false, null),
  ('bad8b5b3-bcb7-5abd-8b39-9498553f4870', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'assignments', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant (38B5O) — Generating Force Assignments', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'First Sergeant, Sr. Career Management NCO (CA Personnel Proponent SWCS), Chief Instructor/Writer, CA Leader Development Noncommissioned Officer, CA Doctrine Development/Writer Noncommissioned Officer, CA Training Development/Writer Noncommissioned Officer, Team Sergeant, CMF Manager.', '7ac2eecf43985c9a61c2d8818b6516965e2ccfe4c8f555bbcd819db55ec1e509', 38, 38, null, 137, null, null, false, null),
  ('42a4acd2-d67b-52cf-94ed-90a387142bfa', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant (38B5O) — Proponent note', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'Master Sergeants should serve in Operational Force at their current grade to gain experience before being assigned to the Generating/Institutional/Training Force.', '17b6e357d3858c9c66db2d123d82544f7d1c11934472d3abf1d473fffaff78fb', 38, 38, null, 138, null, null, false, null),
  ('b37882b4-78fb-5aa3-af61-bb04bd9eb64a', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'development', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant (38B5O) — Proponent note', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'Special consideration should be given to AGR Master Sergeants who are selected to serve as First Sergeant in SWCS or are assigned as the CA CMF 38 Sr. Career Management NCO (CA Personnel Proponent SWCS) and the Training Management NCO billet at USACAPOC(A) HQs.', 'cc9972f97ce6ccae28c17e06a46c51ff04771bc7f20051b981837d6f8ee720cc', 38, 38, null, 139, null, null, false, null),
  ('97c6d93f-49a3-5303-9738-4646b04127bc', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'training', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant (38B5O) — Professional Military Education', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'U.S. Army Sergeants Major Course.', 'fe979a4ac0f096120c3a88ec644a4fe537492a1328f0120fafff3638ef045803', 38, 38, null, 140, null, null, false, null),
  ('8370dadb-d38e-5b63-b52d-76ab9a90f285', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'training', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant (38B5O) — Functional Training', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'Battle Staff Noncommissioned Officer Course, Master Resiliency Trainer Course, Master Fitness Trainer Course, Static Line Jumpmaster course (for airborne assignments), and other ASI/SQI Producing Courses. USAR Soldiers should also pursue job specific and functional courses that enhance professional technical skills and knowledge at various schools including the Army Reserve Readiness Training Center (ARRTC).', '0ae99cfff85ca86559e53c54f19a62e435f05d4e41fa4147632817d69678b12f', 38, 38, null, 141, null, null, false, null),
  ('f7bb1d8a-42c5-518b-80ae-ab8589cf0010', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'lifelong_learning', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant (38B5O) — Lifelong Learning', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'Army Continuing Education System, Army Correspondence Course Program, Army University, Defense Activity for Non-Traditional Education Support, Army IgnitED, Joint Service Transcript, completed bachelor’s degree and working towards a master’s degree. Training at the Center for Excellence in Disaster Management and Humanitarian Assistance is also encouraged. These courses include but are not limited to: Humanitarian Assistance Response Training, Health Emergencies in Large Populations, Health and Humanitarian Action in Emergencies.', '6593b004f9d05cea7e2dfc593b493b37a7f0c9a2c48c7e8bf89f0b0bd5341a90', 38, 38, null, 142, null, null, false, null),
  ('56222887-8f0d-500d-b1ec-6b78a95eeace', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'promotion_criteria', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant (38B5O) — promotion consideration', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'k. Proponent guidance:
(1) Most Qualified (MQ).
(a) TPU Master Sergeants that displayed superior performance while serving 24 months as a First Sergeant. AGR Master Sergeants that displayed superior performance while serving 24 months as a First Sergeant in D/3/1 SWTG (A) or A/5/1 SWTG (A), Sr. Career Management NCO (CA Personnel Proponent SWCS), Chief Instructor/Writer, or Team Sergeant of a Civil Affairs Planning Team in a CA BDE/CACOM and Joint (J5) Senior Enlisted Reserve Liaison.
(b) Physical Fitness. Must meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and pass the AFT with a total score of 450 or better with a minimum of 90 points in each event IAW Army Directive 2025-06.
(c) A pattern of clearly stated strong, quantifiable ratings and enumerations by the Senior Rater that denotes promote ahead of peers and displays the superior potential to serve at the next higher grade.
(2) Fully Qualified (FQ).
(a) TPU Master Sergeants that met satisfactory performance standards while serving 24 months as a First Sergeant. AGR Master Sergeants that displayed outstanding performance while serving 24 months as a First Sergeant, Sr. Career Management NCO (CA Personnel Proponent SWCS), Chief Instructor/Writer, or Team Sergeant of a Civil Affairs Planning Team in a CA battalion.
(b) Physical Fitness: Must meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and pass the AFT with a total score of 300 or better with a minimum of 60 points in each event IAW Army Directive 2025-06 or be on a current profile.
(c) A pattern of clearly stated satisfactory, quantifiable ratings and enumerations by the Senior Rater that denotes promote with peers and displays potential to serve at the next higher grade.
(3) Not Fully Qualified (NFQ).
(a) TPU Master Sergeants that displayed below average performance while serving 24 months as a First Sergeant. AGR Master Sergeants that displayed average performance while serving 24 months as a First Sergeant, Sr. Career Management NCO (CA Personnel Proponent SWCS), Chief Instructor/Writer, or Team Sergeant of a Civil Affairs Planning Team in a CA battalion.
(b) Physical Fitness. Does not meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and/or has not passed the AFT with a total score of 300 or better with a minimum of 60 points in each event IAW Army Directive 2025-06 or be on a current profile.
(c) A clear articulation by the senior rater that denotes to only promote if there is a requirement with average quantifiable ratings and enumerations. Displays average potential to serve at the next higher grade.
(4) Not Fully Qualified for Retention (NFQ-R).
(a) The following must have occurred within the current grade and the 18 months prior to the most current evaluation board:
(1) Leadership: Received a GOMOR or conviction by court-martial in current grade or received a Bar to continued service IAW AR 601-28.
(2) Received NCOER in current grade with any of the following:
(a) Relief for Cause.
(b) Annotation of “Did Not Meet Standard” in Part IV (Performance Evaluation, Professionalism, Attributes, and Competencies), block c (Character).
(c) Annotation of “Not Qualified” in Part V (Senior Rater Overall Potential), block a (Rated Noncommissioned Officer’s Potential).
(3) Assignments: Declines to serve on a CA unit of action, directed assignment, or is unable to meet assignment requirements due to loss of security clearance, flags, or adverse actions.
(4) Military Education: Refuses to complete PME, and/or consistently fails to meet the prerequisites to attend PME or advance skill courses.
(5) Physical Fitness: Fails to score 300 points with a minimum of 60 points in each event on the AFT IAW Army Directive 2025-06, effective 1 June 2025, and/or fails to meet height / weight requirements IAW AR 600-9.', '49fdbb08543ccd60e81e7edbda4b5e8f99781bdc76bf37dd707fd108f4bf4b79', 39, 40, null, 143, null, null, false, null),
  ('8ed5502c-943b-5b1f-a9cd-5a8e4a751dc9', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'assignments', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major (38B6O) — Operational Assignments', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', '(1) The Critical Leadership Development position for a TPU Sergeant Major is serving as an Operations Sergeant Major at the command/brigade/battalion level or as a Command Sergeant Major of a CA battalion.
(2) The Critical Leadership Development positions for an AGR SGM in the operational force is the Chief Advisor at USAJFKSWCS, Operations SGM at USACAPOC or General Officer Level Command, and United States Army Noncommissioned Officer Academy (USANCOA) instructor at the NCOCoE, and the Army Reserve Proponent Advisor (ARPA).', 'da8214bb71c3a5203b82fa7f6c6ec6a2f8f53f74bcf741a006169b92b752d74b', 40, 40, null, 144, null, null, false, null),
  ('8b16d5f4-006d-5aac-98d3-a982346a0bf1', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'assignments', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major (38B6O) — Developmental Assignments', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'Operations Sergeant Major.', 'ff902dab8920e4d0fa4b4458e464f522478bae6fdd5f2d0640ef6b4f7ae4300e', 40, 40, null, 145, null, null, false, null),
  ('5f6b0956-4445-55d0-92b4-c7193f12e837', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'assignments', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major (38B6O) — Generating Force Assignments', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'Chief Instructor/Writer, Chief Advisor, Non-Resident USANCOA Instructor, USANCOA Fellowship.', 'cfd6e7e287456a2a231517d2582c5b29a6e8dc224c3a3c3d5d02cb7a5276b146', 40, 40, null, 146, null, null, false, null),
  ('32003c37-8715-562e-afd4-839c73f4d200', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'training', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major (38B6O) — Professional Military Education', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'Nominative Leader Course.', '6f8b8dcd0d856af71de898a3c127c61ed5cd7c5c660dd19971ecbc3f302a485a', 40, 40, null, 147, null, null, false, null),
  ('7909d397-41da-5bf1-9ba4-7052595f8394', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'training', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major (38B6O) — Functional Training', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'Battalion and Brigade Pre-Command Course (BBPCC) and How the Army Runs Course (HTARC).', '6a532ac35efd3899b26ae44c36b56e5664bb2e70bbb3e427bcb92279aad700e4', 40, 40, null, 148, null, null, false, null),
  ('42a8ae7e-a16e-5e09-909d-d1179c8d97e7', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'lifelong_learning', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major (38B6O) — Lifelong Learning', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'Army Continuing Education System, Army Correspondence Course Program, Army University, Defense Activity for Non-Traditional Education Support, Army IgnitED, Joint Service Transcript, working toward master’s degree. Training at the Center for Excellence in Disaster Management and Humanitarian Assistance is also encouraged. These courses include but are not limited to: Humanitarian Assistance Response Training, Health Emergencies in Large Populations, Health and Humanitarian Action in Emergencies.', 'b83cbec86f75c9f8a81a0993f7da358943529666610f190cb19c7403bb4888b0', 40, 40, null, 149, null, null, false, null),
  ('75b526f1-34c0-5b57-ac06-a4095d6cb0d8', '475beb44-67e2-5ae6-aa28-5f6e03a2be3a', 'Army', 'enlisted', '38B', null, 'promotion_criteria', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major (38B6O) — promotion consideration', 'Chapter 6. Military Occupational Specialty 38B Civil Affairs Specialist', 'j. Proponent guidance:
(1) Most Qualified (MQ).
(a) Must have exceeded performance standards while serving 24-months rated time as a Command Sergeant Major (TPU) or 24-months rated time as an Operations SGM, Chief Advisor, or USANCOA Instructor (AGR).
(b) Physical Fitness. Must meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and pass the AFT with a total score of 450 or better with a minimum of 90 points in each event IAW Army Directive 2025-06.
(c) A pattern of clearly stated strong, quantifiable ratings and enumerations by the Senior Rater that denotes only a top few display the superior potential to serve at the next higher position.
(2) Fully Qualified (FQ).
(a) Must have displayed satisfactory performance while serving 24-months rated time as a Command Sergeant Major or Operations Sergeant Major at the battalion level or higher (TPU) or as an Operations SGM, Chief Advisor, or USANCOA Instructor (AGR).
(b) Physical Fitness. Must meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and pass the AFT with a total score of 300 or better with a minimum of 60 points in each event IAW Army Directive 2025-06 or be on a current profile.
(c) A pattern of clearly stated satisfactory, quantifiable ratings and enumerations by the Senior Rater that denotes promote with peers and displays potential to serve at the next higher position.
(3) Not Fully Qualified (NFQ).
(a) Must have displayed below average performance while serving 24-months rated time as a Command Sergeant Major or Operations Sergeant Major as the battalion level or higher.
(b) Physical Fitness. Does not meet Height and Weight IAW AR 600-9 and/or has not passed the AFT with a total score of 300 or better with a minimum of 60 points in each event IAW Army Directive 2025-06 or be on a current profile.
(c) A clear articulation by the senior rater that denotes to only promote if there is a requirement with average quantifiable ratings and enumerations. Displays average potential to serve at the next higher position.
(4) Not Fully Qualified for Retention (NFQ-R).
(a) The following must have occurred within the current grade and the 18 months prior to the most current evaluation board:
(1) Leadership: Received a GOMOR or conviction by court-martial in current grade or received a Bar to continued service IAW AR 601-28.
(2) Received NCOER in current grade with any of the following:
(a) Relief for Cause.
(b) Annotation of “Did Not Meet Standard” in Part IV (Performance Evaluation, Professionalism, Attributes, and Competencies), block c (Character).
(c) Annotation of “Not Qualified” in Part V (Senior Rater Overall Potential), block a (Rated Noncommissioned Officer’s Potential).
(3) Assignments: Declines to serve on a CA unit of action, directed assignment, or is unable to meet assignment requirements due to loss of security clearance, flags, or adverse actions.
(4) Military Education: Refuses to complete PME, and/or consistently fails to meet the prerequisites to attend PME or advance skill courses.
(5) Physical Fitness: Fails to score 300 points with a minimum of 60 points in each event on the AFT IAW Army Directive 2025-06, effective 1 June 2025, and/or fails to meet height / weight requirements IAW AR 600-9.', '7c642406441b8939c3d1ae0ee55fea14d6eb6d8f7092972cee0ee7f68c75b5bd', 40, 41, null, 150, null, null, false, null);
